\email{corey.omeara@eon.com}
\affiliation{E.ON Digital Technology GmbH, Germany}

\begin{abstract}
The Minimum Birkhoff Decomposition (MBD) seeks a sparse weighted sum of matchings and is a challenging optimization problem with applications in network scheduling and energy trading. We scale a quantum-assisted decomposition method by combining single-layer QAOA sampling with Extended Fully-Corrective Frank-Wolfe (E-FCFW) optimization, spectral graph partitioning, and greedy feasibility repair. We demonstrate the pipeline on the 1,354-node PEGASE bus test case (representing a 1,354-bus transmission grid with 1,710 transmission lines), whose 1,710 edges define a native 1,710-variable matching optimization problem requiring 1,710 qubits in the unpartitioned encoding. Partitioning enables distributed execution on IBM superconducting quantum processors. The best reported hardware result uses a 50-qubit partition bound, while reducing partitions to 20 qubits degrades convergence in the partition-size comparison. Larger partitions are more demanding for matrix product state (MPS) simulation, and bond-dimension tests show that truncating quantum correlations reduces candidate quality. The results therefore motivate retaining a substantive quantum sampling task within each partition as the overall problem scales. On the PEGASE-1354 benchmark, repaired QAOA samples achieve lower residual decomposition error than both simulated annealing and uniform random sampling baselines, while spatial circuit packing reduces hardware execution time by approximately a factor of three. These results demonstrate that combining spectral partitioning, spatial circuit packing, and classical feasibility repair provides an executable path for deploying gate-based quantum sampling on thousand-variable constrained optimization problems on current hardware.

\end{abstract}

\maketitle

\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:intro}

Network scheduling, optical switching, and peer-to-peer energy trading \cite{mohseni2026evidence, bucher2024evaluating} require weighted demands to be served through compatible sets of connections \cite{blenninger2024q,ganeshamurthy2024next}. Representing each set as a matching gives a graph decomposition problem: reconstruct the demand graph with as few weighted matchings as possible. Fewer matchings reduce switching and reconfiguration overhead. This extends the Birkhoff--von Neumann decomposition from bipartite graphs to general network topologies \cite{brualdi1982notes}.

Minimum Birkhoff Decomposition (MBD) is NP-hard~\cite{dufosse2016notes} and can challenge classical optimization at small instance sizes. Its inclusion in the Quantum Optimization Benchmarking Library (QOBLIB) identifies it as a relevant test problem for progress toward quantum advantage in optimization \cite{koch2026quantum}. The difficulty lies in selecting a sparse decomposition; finding an individual maximum-weight matching remains polynomial-time solvable. Quantum sampling is therefore considered here as a way to enrich the candidate pool of a classical decomposition algorithm.

Building on Extended Fully-Corrective Frank-Wolfe (E-FCFW) \cite{pennington2025boosting}, we use single-layer ($p=1$) QAOA to supply diverse matching candidates and optimize their weights classically. Fixed, offline-calibrated angles avoid variational tuning during execution. Although complexity-theoretic results motivate studying shallow QAOA sampling \cite{farhi2016quantum}, they do not establish an advantage for the instances studied here.

While divide-and-conquer and multilevel methods have scaled unconstrained QUBO subproblems on quantum processors to tens of thousands of variables \cite{maciejewski2024multilevel,li2022large}, our contribution is to scale quantum-assisted constrained optimization for graph decomposition beyond the width of an individual processor. Spectral partitioning reduces circuit width and routing overhead; stitching and greedy repair reconstruct feasible global candidates; and spatial circuit packing executes independent subproblems concurrently. Figure~\ref{fig:partitioning_workflow} summarizes the workflow. We examine its dependence on matrix product state (MPS) bond dimension and partition size, then demonstrate hardware execution on the 1,354-node, 1,710-edge PEGASE bus test case \cite{thurner2018pandapower}, which models a realistic 1,354-bus, 1,710-line European high-voltage transmission network developed by the Pan-European Grid Advanced Simulation and State Estimation (PEGASE) project. The central question is whether partitioning can make execution practical while retaining useful quantum sampling within each partition.

\begin{figure*}[ht]
\centering
\resizebox{\textwidth}{!}{}
\caption{Residual graphs are partitioned into QAOA subproblems and dispatched across IBM QPUs using spatial circuit packing. Samples are stitched, repaired to enforce matching feasibility, and added to the classical E-FCFW active set.}
\label{fig:partitioning_workflow}
\end{figure*}

\section{Problem Formulation}
\label{sec:problem}

In this section, we formulate the problem of decomposing general network demand graphs into sparse linear combinations of valid matchings. We first define the MBD as a cardinality-minimization problem over symmetric doubly substochastic matrices. We then review the Extended Fully-Corrective Frank--Wolfe (E-FCFW) optimization framework, which augments classical Frank--Wolfe steps with diverse candidate sampling to achieve sparser graph representations.

\subsection{Minimum Birkhoff Decomposition}
\label{subsec:mbd}

Let $G(V,E)$ denote a weighted, undirected graph with edge weights $w(e) \ge 0$ representing demand or connection intensity. A subgraph $M \subseteq E$ is a valid matching if no two edges in $M$ share a common vertex. We represent $G$ as a symmetric doubly substochastic matrix $D^* \in [0,1]^{n \times n}$ with non-negative entries satisfying:
\begin{equation}
\sum_{j=1}^{n} D^*_{ij} \le 1, \quad \sum_{i=1}^{n} D^*_{ij} \le 1 \quad \forall i,j,
\end{equation}
with zero diagonal entries ($D^*_{ii} = 0$, no self-loops) and symmetric weights ($D^*_{ij} = D^*_{ji}$).

A matching matrix $P$ belongs to the feasible set $\mathcal{P}$:
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{P} := \{U \in \{0,1\}^{n \times n} \mid U = U^T, \, U \mathbf{1} \preceq \mathbf{1}, \, U_{ii} = 0 \,\, \forall i\},
\end{equation}
where $\mathbf{1}$ denotes the vector of all ones. The MBD problem seeks to minimize the number of constituent matchings needed to reconstruct $D^*$, formulated as an $\ell_0$-norm minimization:
\begin{align}
\min_{\alpha, P_l} \quad & \|\alpha\|_0 \label{eq:mbd_obj}\\
\text{s.t.} \quad & D^* = \sum_{l=1}^{m} \alpha_l P_l, \quad P_l \in \mathcal{P}, \nonumber \\
& \alpha_l \ge 0, \quad \sum_{l=1}^{m} \alpha_l \le 1, \nonumber
\end{align}
where $\|\alpha\|_0 = |\{l : \alpha_l > 0\}|$ counts the non-zero weights across a decomposition of length $m$.
An exact decomposition with $\sum_l\alpha_l\le1$ requires $D^*$ to lie in the convex hull of matching matrices (including the empty matching). On non-bipartite graphs, double substochasticity alone is insufficient; otherwise, the framework below computes a sparse approximation. Even in the classical doubly stochastic setting, minimizing decomposition cardinality is NP-hard \cite{dufosse2016notes}. The hard MBD instances documented in QOBLIB motivate sampling-based approaches beyond greedy decomposition \cite{koch2026quantum,durkovic2017birkhoff}.

\subsection{The Extended Fully-Corrective Frank-Wolfe Framework}
\label{subsec:efcfw}

We seek sparse approximations using the Frank-Wolfe (FW) framework \cite{combettes2023revisiting, valls2021birkhoff}. For graph decomposition, the objective function is the scaled squared Frobenius norm distance between the target demand $D^*$ and the approximation matrix $D$:
\begin{equation}
f(D) := \frac{1}{n^2} \|D - D^*\|_F^2.
\end{equation}
At iteration $k$, standard FW linearizes $f(D)$ around the current iterate $D_k$ and queries a Linear Minimization Oracle (LMO):
\begin{equation}
M^{MW} \in \arg\min_{U \in \mathcal{P}} \sum_{i,j=1}^{n} \nabla f(D_k)_{ij} U_{ij},
\end{equation}
which maps to finding a maximum-weight matching on the residual graph $D^* - D_k$ \cite{edmonds1965paths}.

Fully-Corrective Frank-Wolfe (FCFW) re-optimizes weights over all previously collected matchings in an active set $\mathbb{M}$. Defining the substochastic unit simplex $\Delta_m := \{u \in \mathbb{R}_{\ge0}^m : \sum_{i=1}^m u_i \le 1\}$, the weights are updated via:
\begin{equation}
(\alpha_1, \dots, \alpha_{|\mathbb{M}|}) = \arg\min_{u \in \Delta_{|\mathbb{M}|}} \frac{1}{n^2} \left\| D^* - \sum_{i=1}^{|\mathbb{M}|} u_i M_i \right\|_F^2.
\end{equation}

Deterministic oracle calls can yield similar matchings, motivating additional candidate diversity. The E-FCFW algorithm \cite{pennington2025boosting} addresses this by augmenting the candidate pool at each iteration: alongside the deterministic maximum-weight matching $M^{MW}$ (which supplies a Frank-Wolfe descent direction before cardinality restriction), a sampling subroutine generates $d$ diverse candidate matchings $\mathbb{M}^{\text{sample}}$, updating the active pool as $\mathbb{M} \leftarrow \mathbb{M} \cup \{M^{MW}\} \cup \mathbb{M}^{\text{sample}}$. E-FCFW then enforces an explicit $\ell_0$-norm constraint:
\begin{equation}
\min_{u \in \Delta_{|\mathbb{M}|}} \frac{1}{n^2} \left\| D^* - \sum_{i=1}^{|\mathbb{M}|} u_i M_i \right\|_F^2 \quad \text{s.t.} \quad \|u\|_0 \le k+1.
\end{equation}
The cardinality restriction retains at most $k+1$ positive weights. Weight optimization on a fixed support is a convex quadratic program; support selection remains combinatorial.

\section{Methodology}
\label{sec:methods}

Each E-FCFW iteration partitions the residual graph, samples local QAOA circuits, reconstructs global matchings, and updates the decomposition weights. We describe the essential components here; calibration, reconstruction settings, pseudocode, and scheduling details are collected in Appendix~\ref{app:methods}.

\subsection{QAOA Matching Sampler}
\label{subsec:qaoa}

Each edge $j\in E$ is encoded by a qubit, with $x_j=1$ indicating its selection. For residual edge weights $w_j$, we use the QUBO
\begin{equation}
C(x)=-\sum_{j\in E}w_jx_j+\lambda\sum_{\substack{j\in E,\\k\in\Gamma(j)}}x_jx_k,
\end{equation}
where $\Gamma(j)$ contains edges sharing an endpoint with $j$. The sum follows the ordered-pair convention, counting each conflicting pair twice. The penalty $\lambda=0.2$ discourages collisions; greedy repair subsequently enforces feasibility. Substituting $x_j=(\mathbb{I}-\hat Z_j)/2$ defines the diagonal cost Hamiltonian $\hat H_C$ and the single-layer state
\begin{equation}
|\psi(\beta,\gamma)\rangle= e^{-i\beta\sum_{j=1}^{N}\hat X_j}e^{-i\gamma\hat H_C}|+\rangle^{\otimes N},
\end{equation}
with $N=|E|$. The sampler supplies a pool of candidates rather than a single optimal matching.

Angles are calibrated offline and transferred across related subgraphs, following prior work on QAOA parameter transfer \cite{pelofske2024scaling,pennington2025boosting}. We use $(\gamma_0,\beta_0)=(0.5,-0.5)$ for the 120-node benchmark and approximately $(2.49, 0.16)$ for the PEGASE bus test case. They remain fixed throughout execution, eliminating online parameter optimization.

\subsection{Partitioning and Reconstruction}
\label{subsec:partitioning}

While recursive Kernighan--Lin bisection was evaluated in preliminary benchmarking, multi-way spectral partitioning produced subgraphs with fewer cut edges and superior downstream convergence, and was therefore adopted throughout this work. Multi-way spectral partitioning clusters affinity-matrix eigenvectors using $k$-means and recursively refines oversized clusters. Throughout, $p_s$ denotes the maximum number of edges per partition, hence the maximum circuit qubit count, rather than a bound on graph vertices. Spectral partitioning groups strongly connected regions while limiting the number of connections removed from local Hamiltonians.

The residual graph is repartitioned at each iteration. Cut edges cannot be sampled by that iteration's local circuits, but their residual weights can influence later partitions. This provides a route to recovering omitted connections, without guaranteeing that every edge becomes internal within a finite iteration budget. The global classical maximum-weight matching oracle also remains available to the outer solver.

Local samples are combined by batched Cartesian products or pruned beam search. We tested both methods and used batched Cartesian products, as it maintains sample validity more effectively. Recombination can introduce conflicts at cut vertices shared across partition boundaries, alongside any intra-partition collisions permitted by the soft QUBO penalty. 

To restore feasibility without introducing unselected edges, greedy repair sorts the candidate edge set $\tilde E=\{e_j:x_j=1\}$ by descending residual weight $w(e_j)=(D^*-D_k)_{uv}$ and accepts an edge if and only if both endpoints are currently unoccupied. For $|\tilde E|$ selected edges, this pruning requires $O(|\tilde E|\log|\tilde E|)$ time and guarantees matching feasibility. Because repair alters the sample distribution without adding edges beyond those proposed by the quantum sampler, the experiments compare QAOA and random samples under the identical repair procedure to isolate the contribution of sampling.

\subsection{Classical Update and Hardware Execution}

The best distinct repaired candidates are added to the active pool together with a global maximum-weight matching. E-FCFW updates the nonnegative weights subject to its cardinality bound and removes zero-weight matchings. The loop stops at the target error, a relative-improvement plateau, or the iteration limit. Decomposition length counts retained positive-weight matchings and need not equal the iteration index. Algorithm~\ref{alg:partitioned_qaoa} gives the full workflow.

Qoro's Divi framework \cite{divi2024} coordinates circuit generation, sampling, reconstruction, and execution on \texttt{ibm\_kingston} and \texttt{ibm\_marrakesh}. Qoro's scheduler packs independent circuits into disjoint regions of the 156-qubit Heron lattice, using otherwise idle capacity and buffer qubits between subproblem boundaries to reduce dispatch rounds (Fig.~\ref{fig:partitioning_workflow}).

\section{Experimental Results}
\label{sec:results}

In this section, we evaluate the performance of the distributed QAOA sampling framework on graph decomposition benchmarks. We compare partitioned E-FCFW across varying partition bounds against classical simulated annealing and uniform random sampling baselines, implemented both via MPS tensor network simulation and directly on superconducting quantum processors. We further isolate the impact of classical feasibility repair on solution quality and demonstrate throughput speedups achieved through multi-circuit spatial packing on an IBM Heron device.

\subsection{Experimental Setup}

We evaluate a synthetic Heron-topology graph with 120 vertices and 132 edges, followed by the PEGASE-1354 bus test case with 1,354 vertices and 1,710 edges. The generation of the graphs is detailed in \cite{pennington2025boosting}. QAOA circuits use $p=1$ and 25,000 shots per partition per round. Simulated annealing (SA) \cite{kirkpatrick1983optimization} uses 1,500 reads per partition, each with 1,000 sweeps. Uniform random samples pass through the same stitching and repair as QAOA and SA. These are specified sampling budgets, not matched computational costs. 

Hardware execution relied on heuristic transpilation passes targeting minimal two-qubit gate depth. While active error mitigation techniques—specifically dynamical decoupling using the $X_pX_m$ pulse sequence and gate twirling with 32 randomizations—were evaluated, empirical testing showed they degraded candidate quality on these partitioned workloads. Consequently, hardware runs omitted pulse-level mitigation in favor of depth-minimized compilation coupled with downstream greedy repair (Appendix~\ref{app:calibration}).

All samplers supply the top five distinct valid candidates to E-FCFW. The stopping criteria are normalized error $\epsilon=10^{-6}$ or relative cost improvement at most $10^{-3}$. Standalone greedy Birkhoff decomposition (BKF) is also included as a baseline. Appendix~\ref{app:calibration} gives the calibration and stopping details.

\subsection{Classical Simulation and Hardware Convergence}
\label{subsec:exp_mps}

To classically emulate and diagnose the distributed quantum sampling pipeline, we simulate the QAOA circuits using MPS tensor networks via Maestro \cite{bertomeu2025maestro}. In this representation, the MPS bond dimension $\chi$ bounds the bipartite entanglement entropy across any cut by $\log\chi$, directly controlling the amount of quantum correlation retained in the simulated state. This allows us to systematically probe how truncating entanglement impacts candidate sample quality and downstream decomposition performance. 

We compare final decomposition cost with mirror fidelity: the probability of recovering the all-zero state after simulating a state-preparation circuit and its inverse at finite $\chi$ (Appendix~\ref{app:mirror}). Higher bond dimensions improve both mirror fidelity and final cost in Fig.~\ref{fig:cost-fidelity}(b). Thus, retaining the correlations lost at low $\chi$ matters for this sampling task. This diagnostic measures the limitations of the tested MPS approximation; it does not establish hardness for all classical simulation methods.

Figure~\ref{fig:cost-fidelity}(a) compares hardware and simulated QAOA with the classical baselines. Hardware QAOA tracks the simulated convergence and achieves lower costs than random sampling and simulated annealing. Partitioning at $p_s=50$ retains convergence close to the unpartitioned hardware run, supporting its use to reduce circuit size without losing the useful sampling behavior in this instance. SA makes initial progress but triggers the plateau criterion at round 18, with decomposition length 15; QAOA continues to improve. These observations compare the tested samplers and budgets, rather than establishing a general separation from classical optimization.

\begin{figure*}[ht]
\centering
\resizebox{\textwidth}{!}{}
\caption{Benchmark on synthetic demand graphs inspired by heavy-hex topology ($n=120$, $|E|=132$). (a) Normalized approximation cost versus decomposition length for hardware QAOA, MPS simulation, and classical baselines. All methods except greedy Birkhoff (BKF) supply candidates to E-FCFW. (b) Mirror fidelity and final cost versus MPS bond dimension $\chi$: retaining more entanglement improves simulation fidelity and candidate quality.}
\label{fig:cost-fidelity}
\end{figure*}

\subsection{Partition Size and Circuit Density}
\label{subsec:exp_part}

The bounds $p_s\in\{20,50,80\}$ expose a trade-off between graph fragmentation and circuit complexity. Aggressive partitioning ($p_s=20$) yields uniformly shallow circuits that are readily approximated at low MPS bond dimension ($\chi\le16$), but convergence deteriorates as more edges are cut. Relaxing the bound to $p_s=50$ and $p_s=80$ preserves better convergence while reducing hardware requirements relative to the unpartitioned problem, but circuit depth and routing overhead become heavily dependent on local subgraph topology rather than qubit count alone.

Table~\ref{tab:hardware_depths} makes this topological dependence explicit. An 80-qubit circuit has transpiled two-qubit depth 67, whereas circuits with only 42--52 qubits reach depths 110--169. Because qubits represent edges rather than vertices, two qubits interact whenever their edges share an endpoint vertex. While the synthetic graph is generated on Heron's degree-3 lattice, its line graph (the interaction graph of the edge-qubits) introduces triangles and higher local connectivity. Subgraphs that contain dense loop structures or clustered degree-3 junctions produce tightly coupled line graphs that require extensive SWAP routing when mapped back onto Heron's physical qubits.

Conversely, partitions that form predominantly linear paths or open trees route with minimal SWAP overhead. Consequently, logical two-qubit depth tracks local interaction density rather than qubit count, and directly governs the transpiled routing depth. Recent experimental demonstrations on IBM quantum computers reach two-qubit gate depths of up to 452 with reliable output at this qubit count \cite{hartnett2026fast}. However, qubit count alone is an inadequate partitioning objective: local interaction density and hardware embedding determine execution cost. The next scaling step should control partition density alongside size, retaining enough interactions to challenge classical sampling while keeping routed depth manageable.

