\subsection{Deployment on PEGASE-1354}
\label{subsec:exp_utility}

The PEGASE-1354 transmission graph would require 1,710 qubits under an unpartitioned edge encoding. Spectral partitioning at $p_s=50$ instead produces 41 subgraphs for distributed execution (illustrated conceptually in Fig.~\ref{fig:partitioning_workflow} and detailed in the complete 41-subgraph layout of Fig.~\ref{fig:pegase1354_partitions}, Appendix~\ref{app:deployment}). Spatial packing combines independent subproblems concurrently on a processor; specifically, two 38-qubit and two 34-qubit circuits occupy 144 of the 156 available qubits on \texttt{ibm\_marrakesh}, separated by idle buffer qubits (mapped to physical hardware in Fig.~\ref{fig:heron_circuit_packing}).

\begin{figure*}[ht]
\centering

\caption{Cost convergence of partitioned E-FCFW versus decomposition length on simulation and quantum hardware compared with classical baselines, with and without repair. (a) Partitioning bounds $p_s \in \{20, 50, 80\}$ evaluated via MPS simulation ($\chi=128$), compared with partitioned simulated annealing (SA) and uniform random sampling baselines (all using feasibility repair). (b) Hardware execution on IBM Heron (\texttt{ibm\_marrakesh}) tracking 128-bond-dimension MPS simulation, with spatial circuit packing yielding a $3\times$ execution speedup with minimal degradation in candidate quality. (c) Convergence with and without greedy feasibility repair; discarding invalid matchings without repair stalls QAOA convergence above $10^{-5}$, while uniform random sampling without repair yields zero valid matchings. Random sampling is shown with repair. Cost is $\|D-D^*\|_F^2/n^2$; decomposition length counts retained matchings, not outer iterations.}
\label{fig:hardware-repair-run}
\end{figure*}

Figure~\ref{fig:hardware-repair-run}(a) compares convergence under partition bounds $p_s \in \{20, 50, 80\}$ using MPS tensor network simulation with bond dimension set to $\chi=128$, compared with partitioned SA and repaired uniform random sampling baselines. Partitioning at $p_s=50$ and $p_s=80$ significantly improves on $p_s=20$, which cuts too many edges and stalls early. Under identical $p_s=50$ partitioning, QAOA sampling achieves lower residual decomposition error than simulated annealing and random sampling baselines.

Figure~\ref{fig:hardware-repair-run}(b) evaluates hardware execution and spatial packing on \texttt{ibm\_marrakesh}. Unpacked hardware closely tracks 128-bond-dimension MPS simulation, reaching $1.11\times10^{-6}$ at the 20-round limit. Packed execution stops at its round-17 plateau with error $1.58\times10^{-6}$, while reducing measured execution time by approximately $3\times$. The modest difference in final convergence ($4.7\times10^{-7}$) reflects a practical trade-off: spatial packing constrains each circuit to a fixed sub-region, reducing transpiler choice for high-fidelity qubits and exposing circuits to potential spectator crosstalk, in exchange for a three-fold speedup.

Figure~\ref{fig:hardware-repair-run}(c) isolates the impact of feasibility repair. Discarding invalid matchings without repair stalls convergence near $10^{-5}$, whereas random sampling without repair produces zero valid matchings and fails to make any progress. Random samples with repair improve feasibility but retain residual error above $2\times10^{-6}$, whereas QAOA with the same repair reaches $1.1\times10^{-6}$, demonstrating that QAOA provides structured candidates that repair enhances rather than replaces.

\section{Discussion and Conclusion}
\label{sec:discussion}

Partitioned QAOA sampling extends E-FCFW decomposition to a 1,710-edge grid using circuits that fit current quantum hardware. On the PEGASE-1354 bus test case, QAOA with repair improves on random sampling with the same repair, and spatial packing reduces execution time by approximately a factor of three. These results establish an executable distributed workflow for a problem class whose classical difficulty makes it relevant to the search for quantum advantage.

For that goal, the partitions must retain a useful quantum sampling task. Reducing them to small, easily simulated fragments would remove the reason to use a quantum processor. The $p_s=50$ results provide evidence for this requirement: partitioned hardware retains the unpartitioned convergence behavior, while the bond-dimension study shows that aggressive truncation of quantum correlations degrades candidate quality. This is evidence of a useful regime, not proof of classical intractability or quantum advantage.

Beyond partition width, the underlying topology determines where quantum sampling provides distinct utility. While extremely sparse graphs admit efficient classical solutions and dense topologies incur prohibitive SWAP-routing overhead, intermediate-density modular networks—such as power transmission grids—represent the primary target regime. In these modular architectures, classical heuristics frequently become trapped in frustrated local minima across partition cut boundaries, whereas shallow QAOA states maintain the candidate diversity necessary to sustain Frank-Wolfe convergence.

The next step is to target partitions of at least 100 qubits and vary their density systematically: enough connectivity to preserve useful quantum correlations, with transpiled two-qubit depth low enough for reliable execution. Table~\ref{tab:hardware_depths} shows why width and density must be optimized together. 

Preliminary work towards this has been carried out on three benchmark instances from QOBLIB's MBD track up to 4,096 variables~\cite{koch2026quantum}, using partitioned QAOA executed on IBM Heron hardware. The initial results show that the method consistently reaches the target tolerance within 15 outer iterations and achieves substantially sparser decompositions (retaining 50 to 75 matchings) than classical greedy baselines such as Birkhoff+ (which required over 250 matchings). Future work will extend this evaluation across the full benchmark suite with comprehensive runtime accounting and comparisons against strong classical solvers. The objective is to preserve a useful quantum contribution within each partition as the overall problem grows.

