\section{Extended Methods and Hardware Layouts}
\label{app:methods}

\subsection{Parameter Calibration and Sampling Budgets}
\label{app:calibration}

Offline training uses small prototypes ($N\le16$) with similar topology, degree distribution, and edge-weight encoding to the target family. The prototype calibration requires approximately 100 parallel circuits and about ten minutes of MPS simulation in the reported setup. The resulting angles are transferred to larger circuits instead of being optimized at each E-FCFW iteration.

For the PEGASE bus test case, the calibration landscape in Fig.~\ref{fig:beta_gamma_heatmap} is evaluated on a representative 49-qubit partition with Maestro at bond dimension 128, scanning $\gamma \in [-\pi, \pi]$ and $\beta \in [0, \pi/4]$. Its marked minimum near $(\gamma_0, \beta_0) = (2.49, 0.16)$ supplies fixed execution angles [with an equivalent symmetric basin near $(-0.65, 0.16)$ due to $\pi$-periodicity in $\gamma$]. Finite bond dimension makes this an approximate MPS calibration; bond dimension 128 alone does not guarantee exact simulation of a 49-qubit circuit.

SA uses a geometric cooling schedule with 1,000 sweeps per read and 1,500 reads per partition per round, totaling $1.5\times10^6$ sweeps per partition. In preliminary tuning, increasing the sweep count beyond 1,000 yielded diminishing energy improvements due to thermalization saturation, while generating 1,500 independent Markov chain reads provided comparable serial CPU runtime to the acquisition window of 25,000 quantum measurement shots. Because quantum projective measurements sample independent configurations without sequential equilibration sweeps, the shot count reflects hardware execution throughput rather than matched algorithmic operations. Candidate ranking and the outer stopping criteria are shared across sampling methods.

\begin{figure*}
\centering
\begin{minipage}[t]{0.48\textwidth}
  \centering

  \caption{PEGASE-1354 partitioned into 41 subgraphs with edge bound $p_s=50$. Colors distinguish neighboring partitions; gray lines are internal edges and dashed red lines are cut edges. Shaded regions illustrate individual partitions.}
  \label{fig:pegase1354_partitions}
\end{minipage}\hfill
\begin{minipage}[t]{0.48\textwidth}
  \centering

  \caption{Spatial packing on the 156-qubit IBM Heron processor \texttt{ibm\_marrakesh}. Two 38-qubit and two 34-qubit circuits occupy 144 qubits in disjoint regions. Idle qubits and unused cross-boundary couplers separate the circuits.}
  \label{fig:heron_circuit_packing}
\end{minipage}
\end{figure*}

During hardware benchmarking on \texttt{ibm\_kingston} and \texttt{ibm\_marrakesh}, we evaluated dynamical decoupling \cite{viola1999dynamical} with an $X_pX_m$ pulse sequence and Pauli gate twirling with 32 randomizations \cite{ezzell2023dynamical}. However, for shallow $p=1$ QAOA sampling across distributed partitions, the additional pulse overhead and circuit duration increased effective decoherence and sampling variance without improving the downstream repaired candidate quality. We therefore used Qiskit's heuristic transpilation passes (optimization level 3) focused strictly on minimizing SWAP routing and two-qubit depth, leaving feasibility enforcement to the classical greedy repair routine.

Writing $c_k=\|D_k-D^*\|_F^2/n^2$, the loop stops when $c_{k+1}\le10^{-6}$, when $|c_{k+1}-c_k|/c_k\le10^{-3}$ for $c_k>0$, or after 20 iterations. Decomposition length is $|\{l:\alpha_l>0\}|$. Thus a run's round count and its final horizontal coordinate in the convergence plots need not coincide.

\begin{algorithm}
\small
\SetAlCapSkip{3pt}
\caption{Partitioned E-FCFW Pipeline}
\label{alg:partitioned_qaoa}
\SetAlgoLined
\KwIn{Demand matrix $D^*$, convergence tolerance $\epsilon$, max iterations $K$, sample count $d$, partition bound $p_s$.}
\KwOut{Sparse decomposition $\{(\alpha_l, M_l)\}_{l=1}^{|\mathbb{M}|}$.}
Initialize $\mathbb{M} \leftarrow \emptyset$, $D_0 \leftarrow 0$\;
\For{$k = 0, 1, \dots, K-1$}{
    Construct residual graph $G_k$ from $D^* - D_k$\;
    Partition $G_k$ into subgraphs $\{G_1, \dots, G_m\}$ with at most $p_s$ edges using recursive spectral partitioning\;
    \For{\textbf{each} subgraph $G_i$ \textbf{in parallel}}{
        Formulate QUBO Hamiltonian $\hat{H}_{C,i}$\;
        Execute shallow QAOA circuit with pre-selected parameters $(\gamma, \beta) = (\gamma_0, \beta_0)$; sample candidate set $S_i$\;
    }
    Recombine local samples via beam search or Cartesian stitching\;
    Apply greedy repair; retain the top $d$ distinct matchings as $\mathbb{M}^{\text{sample}}$\;    
    Compute a global maximum-weight matching $M^{MW}$ on $G_k$\;
    Update $\mathbb{M} \leftarrow \mathbb{M} \cup \{M^{MW}\} \cup \mathbb{M}^{\text{sample}}$\;
    Solve cardinality-constrained QP over $\mathbb{M}$ to update weights $\alpha$ and iterate $D_{k+1}$\;
    Prune matchings with zero weight ($\alpha_l = 0$) from active set $\mathbb{M}$\;
    \If{$\|D_{k+1} - D^*\|_F^2 / n^2 \le \epsilon$ \textbf{or} cost improvement has plateaued}{
        \textbf{break}\;
    }
}
\end{algorithm}

\subsection{Stitching and Feasibility Repair}

Cartesian stitching evaluates cross-products in local batches capped at $5^5$ combinations per step. The alternative beam search groups subgraphs hierarchically, retains at most 200 candidates per group, and prunes pairwise combinations back to this limit at each merge-tree level. Both methods limit the otherwise exponential number of combinations.

Raw stitched candidates frequently violate matching feasibility due to two distinct sources of collisions: (i) intra-partition collisions, where shallow circuits and finite QUBO penalties ($\lambda=0.2$) permit adjacent edges to be sampled within a partition; and (ii) boundary collisions, where independent subproblems select edges incident on the same shared cut vertex across partition boundaries.

Feasibility repair resolves these conflicts via a greedy pruning pass over the candidate edge set $\tilde E = \{e_j \in E : x_j = 1\}$ extracted from the stitched bitstring:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item The edges in $\tilde E$ are sorted in descending order of current residual weights $w(e_j) = (D^* - D_k)_{uv}$, with ties broken deterministically by edge index.
    \item An initially empty matching $M \leftarrow \emptyset$ and an occupied vertex set $V_{\text{occ}} \leftarrow \emptyset$ are initialized.
    \item Each edge $e = (u,v) \in \tilde E$ is evaluated in sorted order: if $u \notin V_{\text{occ}}$ and $v \notin V_{\text{occ}}$, the edge is accepted ($M \leftarrow M \cup \{e\}$) and its endpoints are marked ($V_{\text{occ}} \leftarrow V_{\text{occ}} \cup \{u,v\}$); otherwise, it is discarded.
\end{enumerate}
Unselected edges ($x_j=0$) are never introduced, ensuring repair acts strictly as a conflict-resolution filter on the quantum candidate rather than an augmenting heuristic. The procedure runs in $O(|\tilde E|\log|\tilde E|)$ time and guarantees matching feasibility. While repair alters the raw sampling distribution, candidate quality is strictly bounded by the edge set $\tilde E$ proposed by the quantum sampler, which is why structured QAOA samples outperform uniform random samples under the identical repair procedure.

\subsection{MPS Mirror Diagnostic}
\label{app:mirror}
We assess MPS truncation error using Maestro's native mirror fidelity check (\texttt{maestro.mirror\_fidelity}), which implements a deterministic compute-uncompute diagnostic conceptually related to the mirror-circuit principle \cite{proctor2022measuring}.

For a QAOA state-preparation circuit $U$ on $N = |E| = 132$ qubits (comprising initial Hadamard gates applied to $|0\rangle^{\otimes N}$ followed by the parameterized cost and mixer layers), Maestro appends the adjoint circuit $U^\dagger$ in reverse order. In exact execution, $U^\dagger U |0\rangle^{\otimes N} = |0\rangle^{\otimes N}$, returning the initial state with unit probability. Under MPS simulation at bond dimension $\chi$, singular-value truncations during both forward and reverse passes introduce approximation errors that reduce the return probability.
Because full statevector inner products are intractable on 132 qubits, Maestro evaluates this diagnostic empirically via shot-based sampling in the computational basis. Over $S = 15{,}000$ measurement shots, the mirror fidelity is the observed frequency of the all-zero bitstring:
\begin{equation}
\hat{\mathcal F}_{\mathrm{mirror}}(\chi) = \frac{N_{0^{\otimes N}}}{S},
\end{equation}
where $N_{0^{\otimes N}}$ is the count of shots yielding $0^{\otimes N}$. Departures from unity diagnose entanglement truncation in the MPS representation. As noted in Section~\ref{subsec:exp_mps}, this return probability characterizes the accuracy of the MPS approximation on the compute-uncompute trajectory rather than certifying the forward state directly, and is analyzed alongside downstream optimization cost.

