\subsection{Orchestration and Circuit Scheduling}

Divi represents residual subgraphs as \texttt{MaxWeight}\allowbreak\texttt{MatchingProblem} instances within a \texttt{Partitioning}\allowbreak\texttt{ProgramEnsemble}, dispatches them through \texttt{QoroService}, and coordinates reconstruction and repair. Qoro's scheduler orders circuits by size and packs them into execution rounds with a shared shot count. It can replicate circuits with large shot requirements on remaining qubits and adjust shot allocation to avoid sparsely filled final rounds. Disjoint register regions and idle buffer qubits separate packed circuits. This reduces dispatch rounds by up to $4\times$ in the reported configurations; the observed PEGASE execution-time reduction is approximately $3\times$.

\subsection{Partition and Packing Layouts}
\label{app:deployment}

Figures~\ref{fig:pegase1354_partitions} and \ref{fig:heron_circuit_packing} expand the partitioning and packing stages of the workflow illustrated in Fig.~\ref{fig:partitioning_workflow}. The PEGASE-1354 layout shows 41 subgraphs, with a four-coloring of the quotient graph to distinguish the subgraphs, emphasizing the splitting up of the workload at the level of the principal problem. The packing layout places four independent circuits (corresponding to different subgraphs) on one Heron processor to be run simultaneously within the same QPU run for a higher ratio of QPU utilization. Spatial circuit packing was possible in this instance because the samples of one subgraph do not sequentially depend on those of another.

