\begin{abstract}
The Quantum Approximate Optimization Algorithm (QAOA) is a variational quantum algorithm used for solving combinatorial optimization problems, which is thought to be a promising application of near-term quantum hardware. However, high rates of noise in these devices can obscure meaningful results. Therefore, it is crucial to understand and suppress the effects of noise on the algorithm's output. Here, we find that the stability of the algorithm in the presence of noise is dependent on its variational parameters. Using a Pauli noise model, we derive an upper bound on the total variation distance between the noisy and noiseless circuit output as a function of these parameters. Results from noisy simulations support this bound and give insight into the best methods to use for parameter generation in the presence of noise. We also introduce new methods of optimization using error penalties and truncated search based on this bound to manufacture error resilience. We extend this analysis to the quantum adiabatic algorithm (QAA) by taking the continuous limit of the QAOA. 
\end{abstract}

\maketitle

\section{Introduction}

Optimization problems are thought to be a significant application of quantum computers, with possible use across fields such as finance, power systems, and transportation logistics~\cite{optop}. Near-term quantum hardware is currently under rapid development and these devices represent an important step toward practical quantum computation~\cite{NISQ}. Limited qubit numbers and relatively high levels of noise, however, make it difficult to realize quantum advantage. Variational quantum algorithms (VQAs) offer a promising approach to achieve this~\cite{vqas,vqe,vqes}. VQAs combine classical and quantum computation, where a parametrized quantum circuit is optimized using classical feedback loops. This structure allows for shallower circuits that are more tolerant to noise, making them especially well-suited for current hardware capabilities~\cite{VQA}. As a result, VQAs have become a central focus in the search for near-term applications of quantum computing.

The Quantum Approximate Optimization Algorithm (QAOA)~\cite{farhi} is a variational algorithm designed to tackle combinatorial optimization problems. Inspired by ideas from quantum annealing and the Quantum Adiabatic Algorithm (QAA)~\cite{qaa}, which evolves a system from the ground state of an initial Hamiltonian to the ground state of a cost Hamiltonian encoding the solution, QAOA can be viewed as a trotterized version of this adiabatic evolution~\cite{farhi}. QAOA operates by alternating between applying problem-specific and mixing Hamiltonians to a quantum state, with the goal of preparing a state that approximates the ground state and hence the optimal solution. 

However, training QAOA on real devices remains a substantial challenge. Noisy hardware can obscure meaningful gradients and make optimization difficult, and the presence of barren plateaus can hinder effective training, especially as the algorithm scales~\cite{BP}. Despite this, QAOA continues to be a candidate for demonstrating practical quantum utility in the NISQ era~\cite{qaoagood}. 

In this work we explore how the choice of variational parameters impacts the resilience of the QAOA circuit to errors. This is a natural question, and indeed  Ref.~\cite{gonzalez2022error} explicitly asked whether specific choices of variational parameters could prevent the proliferation of noise in the resulting circuit. In fact, it is easy to see numerically that the choice of parameters does matter in terms of performance under errors. Fig.~\ref{fig:star} shows the energy produced with and without noise for many sets of parameters, for a simple noise model where we insert one random X or Z error in the circuit. We see that the best parameters in the noise free case are not necessarily the best in the noisy case and there is little correlation between the two. This raises the question of how we should choose parameters when evaluating QAOA on real noisy devices. 

