\end{figure*}

Motivated by this, we undertake a theoretical and numerical study of how the performance of QAOA under errors depends on the choice of parameters. First, we upper bound the total variation distance between distributions gained from circuits with and without errors based on the variational parameters and degree of the graph. 

Averaging over this we obtain an expected upper bound on the total variation distance between the noisy and noiseless distributions that we can use to qualitatively inspect the relative resilience of different sets of parameters. We find smaller valued and smoother varying parameters are more stable in the presence of noise than larger valued, more erratic parameters.

We find that for certain levels of noise taking precomputed parameters, without optimization, is often the most stable approach. While not always performing best in the exact simulated case, these parameters can often provide the best results in the noisy case without the burden of optimization. We construct methods for manufacturing error resilience via additional penalties during optimization and truncating the parameter search space to force smaller valued parameters.

Given the structure of the QAOA parameters that minimize the bound while performing well, we thought it natural to extend this notion to the QAA to find if this gives more insight into suitable QAOA parameter schedules. Taking the infinite time limit of QAOA we recover the QAA and find that our metric of error resilience is lower bounded when using the standard QAA with a single schedule controlling the evolution. We attempt to improve this lower bound by investigating a two schedule approach but find that measures made to attempt to decrease this lower bound increase the runtime of the algorithm such that the bound cannot be improved.

\subsection{Previous Work}

There is evidence to show that QAOA can achieve superior results compared to classical optimization algorithms~\cite{qaoaevidence,qaoaevidence2,qaoaevidence3}. QAOA can also provide a simple but useful model for other quantum algorithms, in particular for quantum simulation~\cite{qaoaforsim}. 

Finding optimal parameters for QAOA is NP-hard~\cite{hardparams}. Methods for the classical optimization of QAOA parameters have been widely studied since noise and time limitations require as few calls to the quantum computer as possible. These include gradient-based and gradient-free methods~\cite{gradient} as well as reinforcement learning~\cite{reinfo}. Similarly, methods are being developed to minimize the need for optimization on hardware irrespective of the classical optimizer, such as warm-starting with accurate guesses for the optimal parameters~\cite{warmstarts,egger2021warm} or parameter prediction strategies which completely bypass the need for optimization~\cite{leo,ieva}.

To utilize the potential of QAOA, it is necessary to ensure the algorithm is robust to the effects of noise. As noted above, VQAs in general tend to suppress noise naturally, as the classical optimization adapts the parameters accordingly~\cite{vqas,fontana2021evaluating}, an effect that has been shown to hold experimentally~\cite{colless2018computation}. Barren plateaus induced by noise in this setting suppress cost gradients exponentially~\cite{BP,BarrenPlateaus}. More recently, work on convergence guarantees under noise has been done for the Variational Quantum Eigensolver (VQE), bounding the shift in the optimal parameters under both coherent and incoherent noise~\cite{resilience_VQEs}. 

For QAOA, work has been done to characterize the effect of noise both numerically and analytically~\cite{xue2021effects, marshall2020characterizing}. This includes proving scaling of output-state fidelity, cost function value, and cost gradient with noise rate, number of qubits and circuit depth yielding an explicit noise-depth trade-off. 

Other results give limits to the possible advantage gained from optimization techniques such as QAOA, including a threshold error rate derived by modelling single qubit error propagation, which is required for the possibility of quantum advantage for VQAs for solving classical optimization problems~\cite{gonzalez2022error}. Similar results were found via relative-entropy inequalities~\cite{stilck2021limitations}. 

\subsection{Organization}

We begin Section \ref{section:preliminaries} by introducing necessary preliminaries including the QAOA, the Max-Cut problem and the QAA and then go on to introduce the noise model we will use throughout. In section \ref{section:theorem} we provide our main result, upper bounding the distance between noisy and noiseless circuit outputs in Theorem \ref{theorem} and provide some numerical results to support this. In section \ref{section:choosing} we investigate the effectiveness of parameters in the presence of noise and introduce new methods of noise-informed parameter selection. Then in section \ref{section:adiabatic} we extend these notions to the QAA. 

\section{Preliminaries}\label{section:preliminaries}

\subsection{QAOA}

Standard QAOA is a VQA with $p$ layers. Each layer consists of a cost and a mixer term with variational parameters $(\gamma_i,\beta_i)$ respectively. Overall, the circuit is parametrized by $2p$ parameters $(\gamma,\beta)$ such that $\gamma = (\gamma_1,\gamma_2,\ldots\gamma_p)$ and $\beta = (\beta_1,\beta_2,\ldots\beta_p)$. 

The goal of QAOA is to find the bitstring(s) $\textbf{z}\in\{0,1\}^n$ that minimize(s) the classical cost function $C(\textbf{z})$ of the optimization problem. The cost Hamiltonian encodes this as
\begin{align}
    H_C  = \sum_{z\in\{0,1\}^n}C(\textbf{z})\ket{\textbf{z}}\bra{\textbf{z}}.
\end{align}
The mixer Hamiltonian, $H_M$, which does not commute with the cost Hamiltonian, is needed in order to explore the solution space.

The full circuit is then
\begin{align}
    U(\gamma,\beta) = U_p\ldots U_1 \quad\text{where}\quad U_k = e^{i\beta_kH_M}e^{i\gamma_kH_C},
\end{align}
applied to an initial state $\ket{\psi_0}$ to give 
\begin{align}
    \ket{\psi(\gamma,\beta)} = U(\gamma,\beta)\ket{\psi_0}.
\end{align}
The initial state is, in general, chosen to be an eigenstate of the mixer, for example, for the standard QAOA mixer $H_M = \sum_{i=1}^nX_i$ we choose $\ket{\psi_0}=\ket{+^n}$. 

This state is then measured and the output is fed into a classical optimizer. To optimize the angles, we construct an objective function as the expectation value of the cost Hamiltonian with respect to the final state
\begin{align}
    F(\gamma,\beta) = \bra{\psi(\gamma,\beta)}H_C\ket{\psi(\gamma,\beta)}.
\end{align}
The classical optimizer is used to search for the optimal parameters $(\gamma^*,\beta^*)$ such that 
\begin{align}
    (\gamma^*,\beta^*) = \argmin _{\gamma,\beta} F(\gamma,\beta).
\end{align}

There are many variants of the QAOA that modify the standard structure~\cite{variants}. This can be via parameter structure, such as multi-angle QAOA~\cite{herrman2022multi} and FALQON~\cite{magann2022feedback}; via the choice of mixer and/or initial state, such as ADAPT-QAOA~\cite{zhu2022adaptive} and warm-started QAOA~\cite{egger2021warm} or via changes to the problem structure, such as RQAOA~\cite{bravyi2020obstacles}. In this paper, we will focus on the standard form of QAOA though there is scope to explore this analysis beyond this.

\subsubsection{Max-Cut}
In this work, we will focus on applying QAOA to the Max-Cut problem. Max-Cut is a fundamental graph optimization problem that is widely used as an application of QAOA \cite{farhi,leo,crooks2018performance,harrigan2021quantum,wang2018quantum}. It involves partitioning a set of graph nodes into two groups via a cut that maximizes the sum of the weights of the edges crossed. For a graph $G(V,E)$ where $V$ is the set of nodes with $|V| = n$ and $E$ is the set of edges, the goal is then to find
\begin{align}
    C^*_{\text{cut}}=\max_{\textbf{z}\in \{\pm1\}^n}C_{\text{cut}}(\mathbf{z}) = \max_{\textbf{z}\in \{\pm1\}^n}\frac{1}{2}\sum_{(i,j)\in E} w_{ij}\left(1 - z_i z_j\right),
\end{align}
which is equivalent to minimizing the Ising energy
\begin{align}\label{maxcutcost}
C^*=\min_{\textbf{z}\in \{\pm1\}^n}C(\textbf{z})=\min_{\textbf{z}\in \{\pm1\}^n}\sum_{(i,j)\in E}w_{ij}z_iz_j,
\end{align}
where $w_{ij}\in \mathbb{R}$ is the edge weight between nodes $i$ and $j$. Throughout, we will focus on the unweighted case where $w_{ij} =1$ for all $(i,j)$.

Finding the optimal solution is an NP-hard problem~\cite{nphard,korte2011combinatorial}. The best guarantee that has been proven for efficient algorithms solving Max-Cut is from the Goemans–Williamson algorithm~\cite{GW,GW1}, which delivers a solution with approximation ratio 0.878, and under the unique games conjecture this is the optimal bound~\cite{ugc}.

Specifically for the Max-Cut problem, the cost function defined in \eqref{maxcutcost} can be mapped to a Hamiltonian whose ground state encodes the solution to this problem
\begin{align}
    H_C = \sum_{(i,j)\in E}w_{ij}Z_iZ_j,
\end{align}
and the mixer Hamiltonian for this problem is typically
\begin{align}
    H_M = \sum_{i=1}^nX_i.
\end{align}
Some of the work done here would more than likely be generalizable to other Ising type problems, but it is future work to verify this.

\subsubsection{Adiabatic evolution}

QAOA in the infinite depth limit can implement the QAA, and QAA can provide useful intuition for QAOA’s performance. In general, the QAA works as follows: consider a quantum system that evolves under the time-dependent Hamiltonian $H(t)$ via the Schr\"odinger equation
\begin{align}\label{schro}
    i\frac{d\ket{\psi(t)}}{dt}=H(t)\ket{\psi(t)}.
\end{align} 
Let $\ket{E_k;t}$ denote the instantaneous  eigenstates of the Hamiltonian, $H(t)\ket{E_k;t} = E_k(t)\ket{E_k;t}$, where $E_k(t)$ are the corresponding eigenvalues and $k$ labels the eigenstates, with $k=0$ the ground state.

The quantum adiabatic theorem states that if the Hamiltonian varies slowly enough in $t$, the state of the system will stay close to the instantaneous ground state of the Hamiltonian at each time $t$, provided the gap $g(t) = E_1(t) - E_0(t)$ is non-zero for all $t$ \cite{born1928beweis,kato1950adiabatic}.

In the form used by Roland and Cerf \cite{roland2002quantum} the adiabatic theorem states, assuming a non-degenerate ground state, and a Hamiltonian free of fast driven oscillations \cite{amin2009consistency},
if we prepare the system at time $t=0$ in its ground state $\ket{E_0;0}$ and let it evolve under the Hamiltonian $H(t)$ for time $T$ to $\ket{\psi(T)}$, then
\begin{align}\label{adiabatic-condition}
    |\braket{E_0;T|\psi(T)}|^2 \geq 1-\eta^2,
\end{align}
provided that
\begin{align}
    \frac{\max_{0\leq t\leq T}\bigg|\bra{E_1;t}\frac{dH}{dt}\ket{E_0;t}\bigg|}{\min_{0\leq t\leq T}g(t)^2}\leq \eta.
\end{align}
for $\eta\ll1$. This is the global condition for adiabaticity. Asserting the same criterion on each infinitesimal time interval gives the local condition
\begin{align}
    \frac{\big|\bra{E_1;t}\frac{dH}{dt}\ket{E_0;t}\big|}{g(t)^2}\leq \eta, \label{localadiabatic}
\end{align}
which is required to hold for all $t$ \cite{roland2002quantum}. 

In general the Hamiltonian takes the form 
\begin{align}\label{sym}
    H(t) = s(t) H_{\text{final}} + (1-s(t)) H_{\text{initial}},
\end{align}
for some schedule $s(t)$ where $s(0)=0$ and $s(T)=1$ and $H_{\text{initial}}$ is a Hamiltonian of which the initial state of our system is a ground state and $H_{\text{final}}$ is some Hamiltonian whose ground state encodes the solution to the problem we are trying to solve. In relation to QAOA, $H_{\text{initial}}=-H_M$ and $H_{\text{final}}=H_C$.

To satisfy \eqref{localadiabatic}, it is sufficient to choose s(t) such that
\begin{align}
    \left|\frac{ds}{dt}\right| \leq \eta\; \frac{g^2(t)}{\left|\bra{E_1;t}\frac{dH}{ds}\ket{E_0;t}\right|}.
\end{align}
$s(t)$ is chosen to satisfy these bounds whilst minimizing the total time the algorithm takes.

This form of the adiabatic theorem comes from the first-order estimation of the transition amplitude rather than a rigorous theorem. Roland and Cerf cite Bransden and Joachain's \textit{Quantum Mechanics} \cite{bransden2000quantum} (and an often cited additional source is A. Messiah's \textit{Quantum Mechanics} \cite{messiah1962quantum}), which use leading-order estimates in the non-adiabatic coupling to give the $\bra{E_1}\dot{H}\ket{E_0}/g^{2}$ criterion.

Conditions of this form have been shown to not be sufficient in general \cite{tong2005quantitative}. Rigorous versions carry further terms involving the second derivative of $H$ and higher powers of $\tfrac{1}{g}$. In particular Theorem 3 from \cite{jansen2007bounds} bounds the distance between the evolved state and the instantaneous ground subspace by terms of this form.

However, we use the first-order approximation here because the violations of this condition are argued to arise from resonant transitions between energy levels, so that for Hamiltonians with no fast driven oscillations the original condition holds \cite{amin2009consistency}. The schedules we use are smooth and monotone and for our examples $H(t)$ has no driving frequency, hence this approximation suffices. 

\subsection{Modelling and Characterizing Noise}

Modelling noise exactly can be very challenging~\cite{lidarandbrun} and different quantum devices experience different kinds of noise to varying degrees. In order to approximate and therefore combat this noise, there are various channels that can be chosen to model it. For example, depolarizing noise corresponds to bit and/or phase flips occurring throughout the circuit and obscures meaningful results by driving the state toward the maximally mixed state~\cite{nielsen2010quantum}.

It has been shown that by applying techniques such as Pauli twirling or randomized compiling, arbitrary noise channels can be transformed into effective Pauli channels~\cite{twirling,twirling2,twirling3}. 
\begin{align}
    \mathcal{P}(\rho) = (1-\epsilon)I\rho I + \epsilon_X X\rho X + \epsilon_Y Y\rho Y +\epsilon_Z Z \rho Z,\label{Pauli}
\end{align}
where $\epsilon = \sum_{K\in\{X,Y,Z\}}\epsilon_K$. In particular, when the two-qubit gates are Clifford gates, these methods ensure that the logical circuit remains unchanged while the noise is simplified to a stochastic Pauli form. This process involves averaging the error channel over a set of Pauli operators, effectively symmetrizing the noise so that it behaves like a probabilistic mixture of Pauli errors, which are easier to analyse and simulate. From this point, we assume noise will take the form of Pauli channels given in \eqref{Pauli}. In simulation, this is done by the random injection of single Paulis on each qubit after each layer, sampled according to the probabilities $(1-\epsilon,\epsilon_X,\epsilon_Y,\epsilon_Z)$. 

