        & \gate{\rxlab{R_x(\beta_1)}}
        & \ \ldots\
        &
        & \gate{\rxlab{R_x(\beta_{s})}}
        &
        & \ \ldots\
        &
        & \gate{\rxlab{R_x(\beta_p)}}
        & \meter{}
    \end{quantikz}}
    \caption{$X$ error propagated to the beginning of the circuit changes the signs of parameters on the cost terms on nodes adjacent to the error.}
    \label{fig:qaoa-b}
  \end{subfigure}
  \caption{QAOA circuit diagram with a single Pauli (a) $Z$ error  or (b) $X$ error inserted after layer $s$. This shows how the errors can be propagated to the end or beginning of the circuit, flipping the signs of the parameters on the non-commuting terms.}
  \label{fig:qaoa-both}
\end{figure*}

We start by considering the simple setting where at most one Pauli error occurs within the circuit between layers. We denote QAOA with a single Pauli error $K\in\{X,Y,Z\}$ on qubit $j$ after layer $s$ as 
\begin{align}
    \tilde{U}^K_{j,s}(\gamma,\beta) = U_p \ldots U_{s+1} K_j U_s \ldots U_1.
\end{align}
We first consider the subset $K \in\{X,Z\}$. Commuting the error through one layer of QAOA flips the sign of the angle, $\beta_s$ or $\gamma_s$, of each term that anticommutes with $K$, while leaving the commuting terms unchanged
\begin{align}
    e^{i\beta X}Z &= Z e^{-i\beta X},\\
    e^{i\gamma Z\otimes Z}(X\otimes I) &= (X\otimes I)e^{-i\gamma Z\otimes Z}.
\end{align}

We write $[A,B]_+ \equiv AB+BA$ for the anti-commutator, and define $\chi_{K,P} \equiv \mathds{1}\{[K,P]_+=0\}$ for $P\in\{X,Z\}$. Propagating the Pauli through one QAOA layer gives
\begin{align}\label{big}
    U_s K_j
    &= K_j \hat{U}_s \notag \\
    &= K_j\,
        e^{\,i\beta_s (-1)^{\chi_{K,X}} X_j}\,
        e^{\,i\beta_s \sum_{i\neq j} X_i}  \\
    &\quad\times\,
        e^{\,i\gamma_s (-1)^{\chi_{K,Z}} \sum_{i\in\mathcal{N}(j)} Z_i Z_j}\,
        e^{\,i\gamma_s \sum_{(i,k)\in E'} Z_i Z_k},\notag
\end{align}
where $\mathcal{N}(j)$ denotes the set of neighbouring nodes of $j$ and $E' = \{(i,k)\in E; i,k \notin \mathcal{N}(j)\}$ the set of edges not connected to node $j$. 

It is sufficient to consider errors only between layers. An error occurring within a layer, i.e. between the cost and mixer terms, can be propagated either forward or backward in the circuit, through terms it commutes with, such that it is between layers. This is then equivalent to the error having occurred at that boundary.

Commuting the error through to the beginning or end of the circuit gives successive phase flips. If we have a Pauli $Z$ error we can propagate it to the end where we measure in the computational basis where the resulting $Z$ has no effect on the output distribution
\begin{align}
    \tilde{U}^Z_{j,s} &= U_p \ldots U_{s+1} Z_j U_s \ldots U_1\\
    & = Z_j \hat{U}_p \ldots \hat{U}_{s+1} U_s \ldots U_1\label{dragZ}.
\end{align}
If we propagate the $X$ to the beginning of the circuit, it will have the same effect on the layers it moves through. Remembering that our initial state is the plus state, $\ket{+^n}$, this is an eigenstate of $X$ and so the residual operator disappears into the initial state:
\begin{align}
    \tilde{U}^X_{j,s} &= U_p \ldots U_{s+1} X_j U_s \ldots U_1\\
    & =U_p \ldots U_{s+1} \hat{U}_s \ldots \hat{U}_1  X_j \label{dragX}.
\end{align}
Therefore, injecting a single Pauli ($X$ or $Z$) is equivalent to running QAOA with a subset of the variational parameter signs flipped plus a residual Pauli that has no effect on the resulting distribution. 

$Y$ errors can be written as $-iZX$ and so we can propagate each through as though they act individually and drop the constant phase with the $Z$.

This is not to say that only qubits with errors are affected. This propagation results in flipped parameter signs on affected and possibly adjacent nodes that allow for the analysis in the following section. However, we can see that errors propagate as a light cone and affect all other qubits in that light cone (see Appendix \ref{appendix:lightcone}.)

\section{Results}

\subsection{Parameter dependent error resilience} \label{section:theorem}

Define the distance measure $\mathcal{D}(\tilde{U},U)$ as the total variation distance (TVD) between the distributions produced from the noisy and noiseless circuits:
\begin{align}\label{tvd}
    \mathcal{D}(\tilde{U},U) =  \| |\tilde U \ket{+^n}|^2 - | U \ket{+^n}|^2 \|_\text{tvd}.
\end{align}
This distance gives a measure of how much an error will alter the distribution induced by measuring the final state and an indication of how much this will affect the quality of the result. 

The following theorem bounds this distance in terms of the variational parameters.
\begin{theorem} \label{theorem}
Let $C(x)$ be a cost function on graph $\mathcal{G}$ with edge set $E$ and $n$ nodes, where node $i$ has degree $d_i$. 

We consider a QAOA circuit $U$ with $p$ layers and parameters $\gamma= \{\gamma_i\}, \beta = \{\beta_i\}$ for $i\in\{1,\ldots,p\}$. We denote the circuit with a single Pauli error $K \in \{X,Y,Z\}$ acting on qubit $j$ after layer $s$, by $\tilde{U}_{j,s}^K$. The total variation distance (TVD) in \eqref{tvd} is bounded by
\begin{align}
\mathcal{D}(\tilde{U}^K_{j,s},U)
  \leq 2\Big[\,
    &\chi_{K,X}\sum_{i=s+1}^{p}|\sin\beta_i| \\
  + &\chi_{K,Z}\max_{\omega\in\{0,\dots,d_j\}}\sum_{i=1}^{s}|\sin(\omega\gamma_i)|
  \,\Big] \notag\\
  \leq 2\Big[\,
    &\chi_{K,X}\sum_{i=s+1}^{p}|\beta_i|
  + \chi_{K,Z}\,d_j\sum_{i=1}^{s}|\gamma_i|
  \,\Big].\label{DK}
\end{align}
\end{theorem}
We defer the proof to Appendix \ref{appendix:theorem1}.

This is an upper bound on the distance between the two circuits; this is not a performance guarantee but an indication of the robustness of a set of QAOA parameters for a given graph. Specifically, a small value certifies robustness to noise but does not guarantee optimality of the resultant noisy state and a large bound does not prove worst-case error resilience but implies it is possible. 

From this, we see the distance between the circuits in the noiseless and noisy case depends on a subset of the variational parameters as well as the density of the affected graph node, with the particular subset depending on the type of noise present. A $Z$ error accumulates the angles associated with the mixer terms that come after it, meaning it has most effect when it happens early in the algorithm. Intuitively, the final measurement is in the computational basis and therefore immune to $Z$ errors. We would expect then that a $Z$ error has a less damaging effect when it occurs close to this measurement. Conversely, an $X$ error accumulates the cost term angles that precede it and thus has worse effect when it occurs later in the algorithm. Again intuitively, we start in the $\ket{+^n}$ state which is an $X$ eigenstate so we expect it to have less impact. A $Y$ error accumulates both, so is damaging at any point in the circuit. 

This can be seen in Fig.~\ref{fig:tvd} where the distance $\mathcal{D}(\tilde{U}^K_{0,s},U)$ is plotted for $K\in\{X,Y,Z\}$ and varying $s$. It shows that the dependence of the distance on $s$ is itself strongly dependent on $K$. A $Z$ error is most damaging when it occurs early in the circuit and an $X$ error is most damaging when it occurs near the end. The distance when a $Y$ error occurs closely follows the sum of the $X$ and $Z$ distances for all $s$, as expected from \eqref{DK}.

