    \label{fig:tvd}
\end{figure}

The bound also implies that high degree qubits are more fragile to $X$ and $Y$ errors as the cost-term contribution picks up a $d_j$ dependence, whereas sensitivity to $Z$ errors is independent of degree. 

The size of the parameters then determines the size of the bound. Larger parameters push this upper bound above $1$ and hence we do not get a useful bound on the effect of errors. 
Smaller parameters keep the distance bounded below $1$ but reduce the expressibility of the circuit and the effectiveness of the algorithm. 

One may wonder whether this is equivalent to small angles preventing the circuit from moving far from the initial state and hence errors having little effect because of this. We provide numerical evidence in Appendix \ref{appendix:performance} that shows there is a separation between the regime where angles are too small to do anything and where the algorithm can remain error resilient. We also provide an upper bound on the distance the state can move from the initial state which gives further evidence for this separation. 

\begin{figure}[t]
    \centering

    \caption{Noiseless and Noisy parameter landscape for 1 layer QAOA for Maxcut on 8 node 3-regular graph. Stars indicate global minima.}
    \label{fig:landscape}
\end{figure}

Fig.~\ref{fig:landscape} is an example of a $p=1$ QAOA parameter landscape for Max-Cut on an 8 node 3-regular graph. As expected, noise flattens the landscape, but also shifts the optimum toward smaller parameters, the behaviour Theorem \ref{theorem} predicts.

Practically, this gives insight into where different errors should be prioritized. Correcting $Z$ errors in early layers, $X$ in later layers and on high degree qubits helps to mitigate the effect of errors. Similarly, for variants of QAOA with independent angles for individual qubits e.g. MA-QAOA~\cite{herrman2022multi}, keeping parameters smaller on error prone or high degree qubits would increase robustness to errors. 

Although this theorem is stated for single Paulis, this can be extended to multiple errors simply by propagating all errors outwards such that the residual Paulis can be dropped. The distance is then bounded by the sum of all the phase flips caused by all propagations. 

Generalizing to the Pauli noise model as in \eqref{Pauli}, we define $\mathcal{E}(\tilde{U}^K_{j,s} ,U)$ as this upper bound on the distance
\begin{align}\label{upperbound}
    \mathcal{E}(\tilde{U}^K_{j,s} ,U)&=\chi_{K,X}\left[ 2\sum_{i = s+1}^{p}|\beta_i|\right] + \chi_{K,Z} \left[ 2d_j\sum_{i = 1}^{s}|\gamma_i|\right].
\end{align}
We find an upper bound on the expected distance by taking the expectation of \eqref{upperbound} for this error model, writing $\epsilon_{xy} = \epsilon_{x}+ \epsilon_{y}$ and $ \epsilon_{zy}=\epsilon_{y}+\epsilon_{z}$
\begin{align}
    \mathcal{E}(\gamma,\beta) &= \mathbb{E}_{\text{errors}}\left[\mathcal{E}(\tilde{U}^K_{j,s} ,U)\right]\\
    &=2n\sum_{i=1}^p
    \left[\epsilon_{zy}i|\beta_i| + 
    \epsilon_{xy}(p-i+1)d_{\text{avg}}|\gamma_i|\right].\label{mathcalE}
\end{align}
From this, we can infer which sets of parameters will provide more stability against general errors. The parameters in the final layers have more weight for $Z$ errors as it is always the parameters after the error that add to the distance. The converse is true for $X$ errors. This points toward ramping schedules, where $\gamma_i$ start small and increase and $\beta_i$ begin large in magnitude and decrease, which resemble adiabatic schedules and inherently suppress $\mathcal{E}(\gamma,\beta)$.

This gives us a quantity to measure the resilience of a circuit with parameters $(\gamma,\beta)$ to Pauli noise. The aim would then be to minimize this quantity while also minimizing the energy of the resulting state to balance the quality of the solution with robustness to noise. 

\subsubsection{Numerical results}

\begin{figure*}[t]
    \centering

    \caption{a) Noisy and noiseless energy, b) energy difference, c) distance and d) upper bound on expected distance for Max-Cut on a 10 node line graph using 3 different parameter generation methods: predicted using \textit{SKatan Method} from~\cite{ieva}; optimized starting from the predicted parameters; and optimized from a random starting point. Both optimizations were done offline, in exact simulation using LBFGS \cite{lbfgs}. Single Pauli $X$ and $Z$ were injected uniformly at a random layer and random qubit in the circuit and the results were averaged over 200 circuit runs.}
    \label{fig:dist}
\end{figure*}

To determine whether this upper bound gives useful insight into the performance of different choices of parameters, we compared 3 different choices of parameters for Max-Cut on a 10 node line graph for various numbers of QAOA layers $p$ and evaluated the noise resilience through the quantity $\mathcal{E}(\gamma,\beta)$. The predicted parameters are small, smooth, and monotonic whereas those optimized from random are large, jagged, and irregular. Noise was simulated using randomly injected Paulis after each QAOA layer.

The results in Fig.~\ref{fig:dist} show that not all parameters are equally error resilient. Though the parameters optimized from random perform reasonably well without any noise, they are the worst in the noisy case. Conversely, the predicted parameters perform worst in the noiseless case but perform similarly to optimized parameters in the noisy regime, perhaps suggesting optimization offers little benefit if noise is not taken into account. The difference in energy is tracked by the TVD \eqref{tvd}, justifying this as a good quantity to bound. We see a qualitative correlation between the upper bound on the distance and the actual distance. The predicted parameters maintain a much smaller distance from the noiseless state and the expected upper bound predicts this. Quantitatively, the bound is loose, but its qualitative agreement with $\mathcal{D}(\tilde U,U)$ and $\Delta E$ makes it a good qualitative indicator of relative noise resilience across the methods.

\subsection{Noise-informed parameter selection}\label{section:choosing}

Building on results in the previous section, we set out to devise methods for generating parameters to increase noise resilience and to determine which sets of existing parameter generation methods perform best in the noisy regime. In this section we will firstly present results showing that, depending on the noise rate, predetermined parameters can be more stable to noise than methods which require optimization. We will compare the stability of existing methods of parameter generation and introduce a noise-informed parameter optimization that we find to provide marginal improvements in results in the presence of noise. These preliminary results act as a proof of concept for future work exploring how to make algorithms robust in the NISQ era. Though this paper explores QAOA explicitly, it is possible that these results extend to more general VQAs.

\subsubsection{Stability of Predetermined Parameters}

We look at the approximation ratio achieved by different methods of parameter generation with varying rates of noise in the circuit. We define the Max-Cut approximation ratio 
\begin{align}
    AR(\gamma,\beta) = \frac{\langle C_{\text{cut}} \rangle_{\gamma,\beta}}{C^*_{\text{cut}}} =\frac{W-F(\gamma,\beta)}{W -C^*}.
\end{align}
where the optimal costs $C^*$ and $C^*_{\text{cut}}$ are defined above and $W = \sum_{(i,j)\in E}w_{ij}$.

From Fig.~\ref{fig:noise_rates} we see that at certain noise rates, optimizing parameters without noise is less effective than using these precomputed parameters or optimizing with noise. At low rates of noise, parameters optimized in exact emulation achieve the best approximation ratio across these graphs but beyond $\epsilon \approx 0.01$, optimizing either with knowledge of the noise or without does not improve the approximation ratio one could achieve by taking precomputed parameters without any optimization. This is significant as optimization can be costly, especially on device, therefore if it does not give advantage over precomputed parameters, skipping optimization altogether would yield notable cost savings.

