    \label{fig:noise_rates}
\end{figure}

\subsubsection{Resilient Parameter optimization}\label{section:penalty}

Given the insight from Theorem ~\ref{theorem}, that smaller parameters are naturally more resilient to noise, we can attempt to encourage robustness by introducing penalties that give preference to smaller parameters.

We can add a penalty to the objective function based on the expected value of the upper bound on the distance between noisy and noiseless circuits in \eqref{mathcalE}. The parameter optimization problem then becomes
\begin{align}\label{penalty}
    (\gamma^*,\beta^*) = \argmin _{\gamma,\beta} \bigg[F(\gamma,\beta) + \lambda \;\mathcal{E}(\gamma,\beta) \bigg].
\end{align}
The penalty weight $\lambda$ should be chosen to balance the quality of the solution with the resilience of the parameters. 

Alternatively, since this penalty adds complexity to the optimization process that may be costly, a simpler way to restrict the optimizer to smaller parameters is to truncate the search space. For example, adding the constraints $0\leq\gamma_i\leq\frac{\pi}{2}$ and $-\frac{\pi}{2}\leq\beta_i\leq0$ for all $i$, bounds \eqref{mathcalE} by
\begin{align}
    \mathcal{E}(\gamma,\beta) \leq \frac{np\pi}{2}(p+1)(\epsilon_{zy}+d_{\text{avg}}\epsilon_{xy}).
\end{align}
Thus the optimization problem becomes
\begin{align}\label{truncation}
    (\gamma^*,\beta^*) = \argmin _{\gamma\in[0,\frac{\pi}{2}],\beta\in[-\frac{\pi}{2},0]}F(\gamma,\beta).
\end{align}
This constraint is simple to implement, but risks excluding a global minimum outside the bounded parameter space. 

The periodicity of QAOA parameters varies by problem type and hence the values for truncation may vary. Theorem 2 in \cite{lee2023depth} gives the periodicity of QAOA for unweighted Max-Cut as $2\pi$ in $\gamma$ and $\pi/2$ in $\beta$. Differing conventions in the mixer and cost Hamiltonians mean this translates to a period of $\pi$ in both $\gamma$ and $\beta$ here. Therefore the truncation in \eqref{truncation} shrinks each parameter space by half.

