\subsection{Adiabatic limit of QAOA}\label{section:adiabatic}

The sets of parameters that best minimize \eqref{mathcalE} while still maintaining good performance, are those that resemble adiabatic schedules. That is, the cost parameter ramps up and the mixer parameter ramps down. This inspired an extension of this analysis to the QAA to gain insight into the error resilience of the QAA, or into potential QAOA parameter schedules that perform well in the presence of errors.

We approximate the QAA using QAOA by taking the limit $p\rightarrow\infty$. Here, the layer index becomes a continuous time, $i\rightarrow t \in [0,T]$ and the discrete parameters become functions of time $\gamma_i \rightarrow \gamma(t)$, $\beta_i \rightarrow \beta(t)$. In the standard QAA, as in \eqref{sym}, there is a single schedule $s(t)$ such that $\gamma(t)=1-\beta(t)=s(t)$. Using this, \eqref{mathcalE} becomes
\begin{align}
    \mathcal{E}(s)
    &\approx 2n\int_0^T\epsilon_{zy}t|1-s(t)| + \epsilon_{xy}(T-t)d_{\text{avg}}|s(t)|\,\mathrm{d}t\label{expected}.
\end{align}
We want to find whether this is bounded, in order to learn about the intrinsic error resilience of these schedules.

More generally we want to know if we can do better than this with any schedule. The simplest way to do this is to assume unphysical schedules to upper and lower bound this such that any physical schedule sits between them.

Assuming $0\leq s(t) \leq 1$ and $ds/dt\geq0$, we bound this quantity by choosing extremal values for $s(t)$. 

$\mathcal{E}(s)$ is maximized by the constant schedules,
$s(t)=0$ or $s(t)=1$, whichever is larger depending on the error rates. If instead $s(t)$ is a step function with a step at $t=\frac{\epsilon_{xy}d_\text{avg}T}{\epsilon_{xy}d_\text{avg}+\epsilon_{zy}}$, evaluating \eqref{expected} gives a lower bound. Therefore, we have 
\begin{align}
nT^2 \cdot
\frac{\epsilon_{xy}\epsilon_{zy}d_{\text{avg}}}
     {\epsilon_{zy} + \epsilon_{xy}d_{\text{avg}}}
\leq
\mathcal{E}(s)
\leq
nT^2 \cdot\max\{\epsilon_{xy}d_{\text{avg}},\, \epsilon_{zy}\}
\end{align}
These are unphysical schedules that lower bound $\mathcal{E}(s)$ such that a physical schedule can only do worse. This gives it a fixed floor that scales with $nT^2$, to suppress this below $\mathcal{O}(1)$ requires error rates $\epsilon\lesssim 1/(nT^2)$ which is small once $T$ is fixed by the adiabatic runtime $\sim\sqrt{N}$. This indicates a fixed resilience to errors that cannot be improved if we maintain a single adiabatic schedule. 

To attempt to beat the lower bound on $\mathcal{E}(s)$, we introduce two independent schedules. Instead of $s(t)$ in \eqref{sym}, we have $\gamma(t),\beta(t)$
\begin{align}
    H(t) = \gamma(t) H_{\text{final}} + \beta (t) H_{\text{initial}}.
\end{align}
The symmetric constraint $\gamma(t) = 1-\beta(t)$ limited how small \eqref{expected} could get, since demanding $\gamma = \beta$ at the crossing forces the schedules to meet at a crossing point $\delta = 1/2$. Using individual schedules $\gamma(t)$ and $\beta(t)$, removes this restriction and $\delta$ can be pushed below $1/2$ such that the integral is reduced. Therefore, instead of \eqref{expected} we get
\begin{align}
    \mathcal{E}_{\text{cont}}(\gamma,\beta)
    &\approx 2n\int_0^T\epsilon_{zy}t|\beta(t)| + \epsilon_{xy}(T-t)d_{\text{avg}}|\gamma(t)|\,\mathrm{d}t\label{expected2}.
\end{align}
Again, by assuming unphysical schedules that do not obey the adiabatic constraints, this bound can go to zero, namely individual step functions where $\beta(0)=1$ but $\beta(t>0)=0$ and $\gamma(0\leq t<T)=0$ but $\gamma(T)=1$. 

This frees us from the lower bound placed on symmetric schedules. However, since we assumed unphysical schedules it does not guarantee a physically possible schedule with a better achievable $\mathcal{E}_{\text{cont}}(\gamma,\beta)$ exists. 

This raises the question of whether the two schedule approach has better inherent error performance than the single schedule when physical constraints are imposed, in particular, when we decrease the crossing point $\delta$.

To investigate this we use the adiabatic theorem to examine physically realistic schedules via the example problem of unstructured quantum search. This work follows a similar structure to~\cite{roland2002quantum} and~\cite{isermann2021optimal}, but with two schedules. 

We find that when we build in physically realistic constraints on the schedules and runtime, it is not possible to get significantly better error behaviour. Physical constraints limit the speed at which the schedules can change and hence determine the total runtime of the algorithm. Forcing the schedules smaller increases the runtime. Increasing the duration of the algorithm has more impact on $\mathcal{E}$ than shrinking the schedules and hence we find worse error behaviour. 

\subsubsection{Unstructured quantum Search}

Quantum search for element $m$ using the adiabatic evolution in \eqref{schro} is given by the Hamiltonian
\begin{align}\label{ham}
    H(t) = \gamma(t) H_m + \beta (t) H_0,
\end{align}
where
\begin{align}
    H_m &= I - \ket{m}\bra{m}\\
    H_0 &= I - \ket{+^n}\bra{+^n}.\label{H0}
\end{align}
We impose on the two schedules, assumptions generally made for $s(t)$. Firstly, boundary conditions $\gamma(0) = 0$, $\beta(0) = 1$, $\gamma(T) = 1$,$\beta(T) = 0$. Secondly, the monotonicity of each schedule, meaning they cross at a single point where $\gamma = \beta = \delta$ and that they cannot both vanish simultaneously. Finally, that $\gamma(t)$ and $\beta(t)$ are independent, in particular $\gamma \neq 1 - \beta$. Looking at the form of \eqref{expected2}, and taking inspiration from typical adiabatic schedules, we might assume that a standard adiabatic schedule, with the crossing point $\delta$ pushed lower, would increase the resilience to errors. 

Under these assumptions, for quantum search with 2 schedules the gap $g(t)$ is 
\begin{align}
    g(t)  = \sqrt{(\beta(t)-\gamma(t))^2+\frac{4\beta(t)\gamma(t)}{N}},
\end{align}
where $N = 2^n$ and the numerator on the left hand side of \eqref{localadiabatic} becomes 
\begin{align}
    \big|\bra{E_1;t}\frac{dH}{dt}\ket{E_0;t}\big|=\frac{\sqrt{N-1}}{Ng(t)}|\frac{d\gamma}{dt}\beta - \frac{d\beta}{dt}\gamma|,
\end{align}
the proofs of which are in Appendix ~\ref{appendix:Adiabatic}.

So, using the monotonicity condition, \eqref{localadiabatic} becomes
\begin{equation}
    \frac{d\gamma}{dt}\beta - \frac{d\beta}{dt}\gamma \leq \eta\sqrt{N}\left[(\gamma - \beta)^2 + \frac{4\gamma\beta}{N}\right]^{3/2},
    \label{constraint}
\end{equation}
giving the condition for local adiabatic evolution for \eqref{ham}. Using this, we determine a bound on the total time of the algorithm and therefore bound $\mathcal{E}_{\text{cont}}(\gamma,\beta)$ to evaluate the noise resilience of the two schedule case for quantum search. 

We split the analysis at $(\gamma-\beta)^2= 4\gamma\beta/N$, treating the regime where
$\gamma$ and $\beta$ are far apart separately from the regime near the crossing point $\delta$. 

In the crossing region $(\gamma-\beta)^2 < 4\gamma\beta/N$, and the $\frac{4\gamma\beta}{N}$ term dominates \eqref{constraint} and we get
\begin{align}
     \frac{d\gamma}{dt}\beta-\frac{d\beta}{dt}\gamma \leq \sqrt{N}\eta\bigg(\frac{4\gamma\beta}{N}\bigg)^{\frac{3}{2}}= \frac{8\eta(\gamma\beta)^{\frac{3}{2}}}{N}.
\end{align}

The region is defined by $|\gamma - \beta| < 2\sqrt{\gamma\beta}/\sqrt{N}$, which near the crossing point spans $-2\delta/\sqrt{N} \lesssim \gamma - \beta \lesssim 2\delta/\sqrt{N}$, giving a width
$\Delta \lesssim 4\delta/\sqrt{N}$. At the crossing $\gamma=\beta=\delta$, the rate at which the schedule traverses the region is bounded
\begin{equation}
    \frac{d\gamma}{dt}-\frac{d\beta}{dt}\leq\frac{8\eta\delta^2}{N}.
    \label{rate}
\end{equation}
which in fact holds throughout the crossing region (proof in Appendix ~\ref{appendix:rate_crossing}). The time spent in this region is therefore 
\begin{equation}
    T_{\text{cross}}\gtrsim \frac{\Delta}{\max(\frac{d\gamma}{dt} - \frac{d\beta}{dt})}\approx \frac{4\delta/\sqrt{N}}{8\eta\delta^2/N}
    = \frac{\sqrt{N}}{2\eta\delta}.
\end{equation}

We can lower bound the total time $T$ by the time spent in the crossing region $T_{\text{cross}}$. The total runtime therefore is $T \geq T_{\text{cross}}= \Omega\left(\frac{\sqrt{N}}{\eta\delta}\right)$.

We now lower bound \eqref{expected2} by evaluating it within this region. Let $[A,B]$ be the time interval during which the schedule is within the crossing region, so that $B-A=T_{\text{cross}}$. The crossing region width means $\gamma, \beta \geq \delta(1 - 2/\sqrt{N})$. Restricting \eqref{expected2} to $[A,B]$ and using this lower bound gives
\begin{align}
    \mathcal{E}_{\text{cont}}(\gamma,\beta)
    &\gtrsim 2n\delta\left(1 - \frac{2}{\sqrt{N}}\right) \int_A^B \epsilon_{zy}t + \epsilon_{xy}d_{\text{avg}}(T-t)\mathrm{d}t .
\end{align}
Writing $B^2 - A^2 = (A+B)T_{\text{cross}}$, we bound both terms in the integral separately
\begin{align}
    \int_A^B \epsilon_{zy}\,t\,\mathrm{d}t
    &= \tfrac{1}{2}\epsilon_{zy}(A+B)\,T_{\text{cross}}
    \geq \tfrac{1}{2}\epsilon_{zy}\,T_{\text{cross}}^2 ,\\
    \int_A^B \epsilon_{xy}d_{\text{avg}}(T-t)\,\mathrm{d}t
    &= \epsilon_{xy}d_{\text{avg}}\big[T-\tfrac{1}{2}(B+A)\big]\,T_{\text{cross}},\\&
    \geq \tfrac{1}{2}\epsilon_{xy}d_{\text{avg}}\,T_{\text{cross}}^2 ,
\end{align}
where the first inequality uses $A+B \geq B-A = T_{\text{cross}}$, and the second uses $(T-A)+(T-B) \geq T-A \geq B-A = T_{\text{cross}}$ since $B \leq T$. Together these give
\begin{align}
    \mathcal{E}_{\text{cont}}
    &\gtrsim n\delta\left(1 - \frac{2}{\sqrt{N}}\right)\left(\epsilon_{zy} + \epsilon_{xy}d_{\text{avg}}\right) T_{\text{cross}}^2 .
\end{align}
Substituting $T_{\text{cross}} \gtrsim \frac{\sqrt{N}}{2\eta\delta}$ then gives
\begin{align}
    \mathcal{E}_{\text{cont}}
    &= \Omega\!\left( \frac{nN}{\eta^2\delta}\left(\epsilon_{zy} + \epsilon_{xy}d_{\text{avg}}\right)\right).
\end{align}
This bound grows as $\delta$ decreases. A smaller crossing point lengthens $T_{\text{cross}}$ and the $T_{\text{cross}}^2$ dependence of $\mathcal{E}(\gamma,\beta)$ means the net effect is worse the smaller $\delta$ is.

This intuitively makes sense; a longer runtime $T$ with fixed noise rates means more errors. This is analogous to increasing the number of layers $p$ in a QAOA circuit so we would expect more errors. 

\section{Conclusion}

We find that the choice of variational parameters in the QAOA affects the stability of the circuit in the presence of noise. In fact, using a Pauli error model with single injected Pauli errors, we derive a bound on how far the noisy output state is from the desired noiseless state based on the sum of the parameters. This allows us in turn to compare the stability of methods for generating parameters as well as adding penalties to the optimization method in order to construct an error resilient circuit.

We find that optimizing without consideration of noise (which can be done to avoid costly noisy optimization on device \cite{streif2020training,pelofske2024scaling,maciejewski2026quantum}) can perform worse in the noisy case than unoptimized parameters.

In particular, beyond a certain noise rate precomputed parameters can perform better than optimization methods. Verifying that this scales and is applicable to real hardware noise is left to future work. However, if this shows that optimizing is not beneficial, using precomputed parameters would save optimization costs.

Further work is needed to verify the methods presented here for noise-informed parameter generation that attempt to manufacture error resilience are general and effective and scale beyond small instances. The preliminary results show simply optimizing starting from predicted parameters is the most effective method in this case. 

The stability of non-optimized parameters comes primarily from their shape as the adiabatic-looking schedules -- monotonically increasing $|\gamma|$ and decreasing $|\beta|$ -- tend to minimize the bound and exhibit more resilience to errors. This led us to investigate the adiabatic algorithm in the context of our bound to see if we gain any insight about which are the best QAOA schedules. We found that the single schedule adiabatic algorithm has a fixed floor on how error resilient it can be based on our bound. In an attempt to remove this lower bound we derive a 2 schedule adiabatic algorithm and show this does not help in improving the error resilience of the algorithm.

The bound \eqref{mathcalE} shows us that when the most connected qubits (high $d_j$) have errors, the impact is worse. This may be combated by an MA-QAOA style parameter shrinking on these qubits to counteract errors on them, essentially pre-preparing them for noise. Or similarly, a form of device-dependent parameter scaling may be possible. For example, if certain qubits on device are known to have high error rates, having individual parameters for these and scaling them down to reduce the impact would likely improve the error resilience of the circuit. The question of how this affects performance is open.

Furthermore, the structure of the derivation of this bound means it may be possible to extend this to other VQAs. This bound works because we have two anticommuting alternating operators and errors in the same anticommuting basis, therefore any similarly structured ansatz may have similar behaviour.

Finally, having found that parameters generated without optimization often perform better than parameters optimized without noise, it is an open question as to whether a method of noise-informed parameter prediction using insights from this bound would improve this even further. 

