\section{Introduction}

Quantum error correction is essential for protecting quantum information against imperfect operations and environmental noise. A central challenge is to achieve this protection with resources that remain manageable for large-scale quantum computers \cite{gottesman1998theory,gottesman2010introduction}.
Quantum low-density parity-check (qLDPC) codes offer a promising route to fault tolerance as they ensure that parity-check weights and individual qubit degrees remain bounded independently of the overall code size \cite{BreuckmannEberhardt2021}. 
Many qLDPC codes have constant encoding rates and code distance growing with the system size \cite{BreuckmannEberhardt2021, Terhal2015}. 
Realizing the potential of qLDPC codes, however, requires balancing encoding rate, code distance, check weight, and the physical interactions required to perform syndrome measurements.

However, several theoretical bounds restrict qLDPC codes parameters and control the choices available to design new codes. For a surface code with parameters \([[n,k,d]]\), there is a tradeoff between the encoding rate $k/n$ and the square of the code distance $d^2$. For instance, for codes with bounded interaction range on a two-dimensional Euclidean lattice of fixed qubit density, the Bravyi--Poulin--Terhal (BPT) bound gives \(kd^2/n=O(1)\) \cite{BravyiPoulinTerhal2010}. Thus, a growing distance requires a vanishing encoding rate \(k/n\ \rightarrow 0\) under these assumptions. Conventional Euclidean surface codes obey this bound, but remain attractive blueprints for quantum fault tolerance due to their simple geometric structure \cite{Kitaev2003,Dennis2002,Terhal2015}, established decoding methods \cite{fowler2012towards, delfosse2021almost, bausch2024learning}, developed protocols for logical operations \cite{horsman2012surface, bravyi2005universal, fowler2012surface}, as well as their experimental accessibility \cite{google2023suppressing, krinner2022realizing, zhao2022realization, google2025quantum} (see also Ref.~\cite{Rayan2026} for a 
generalized field-theoretic perspective on topological surface codes). At the same time, their resource overhead motivates the search for more efficient alternatives, with similar geometric, decoding and experimental attainability advantages.

Relaxing geometric locality creates new possibilities, but sparse checks alone do not guarantee favorable code parameters. Baspin and Krishna relate the achievable distance and encoding rate to how readily the qubit connectivity graph can be separated into smaller pieces \cite{BaspinKrishna2022,BaspinEtAl2024}. More concretely, they prove that for any qLDPC code, the code distance is upper bounded by the separation profile of the code's connectivity graph. 

Check weight imposes further restrictions. Stabilizer codes with checks of weight at most three cannot have distance greater than two if they encode any logical information \cite{WangEtAl2026}. For CSS codes, whose checks separate into \(X\)-type and \(Z\)-type operators, a weight-four code in which every qubit belongs to exactly two checks of each type obeys \(kd^2/n=O(1)\), even without assuming Euclidean locality \cite{WangEtAl2026}. Within this structure, exceeding constant efficiency \(kd^2/n=O(1)\) therefore requires checks of weight at least five.
These bounds motivate constructions that retain modest check weights while maintaining rich global connectivity.

Tile codes relax parity-check locality to improve upon conventional surface-code patches while retaining a planar lattice construction~\cite{tile_codes_prl,BreuckmannEtAl2025TileLogical}. 
Concurrently, bivariate-bicycle (BB) and barbell codes leverage modular architectures to achieve highly competitive encoding rates and distances~\cite{BravyiEtAl2024,eberhardt2024logical,eberhardt2024pruning,choe2026barbell}. 
Although their favorable finite-size scaling is highly promising for near-term devices, it does not by itself guarantee advantageous asymptotic scaling. 
Specifically, bounded-range planar tile and barbell families remain fundamentally limited by the BPT bound, $kd^2/n = O(1)$. 
For BB codes, two-layer routing alone does not establish uniformly bounded geometric range~\cite{BravyiEtAl2024}. BPT applicability must therefore be assessed for the particular BB family; any family with bounded stabilizer range on a two-dimensional lattice, even with periodic boundaries, obeys the same bound.
A distinct scaling limitation arises in the Pinnacle architecture, which employs a specific sequence of generalized-bicycle codes~\cite{WebsterEtAl2026Pinnacle,WebsterEtAl2025Gadgets}. For these codes, the parameters $n=2(2^m-1)$ and $k=2m$, combined with the conjectured distance $d=m+(m-4)^2$, yield a vanishing asymptotic efficiency scaling of $kd^2/n = \Theta((\log n)^5/n) \to 0$. 
While replicating fixed-size code blocks successfully preserves the block-wise encoding rate \cite{BreuckmannEberhardt2021}, it inherently fails to scale the code distance. Consequently, these examples underscore a critical challenge in quantum error correction: characterizing how code efficiency $kd^2/n$ scales as the code size increases beyond small code instances, while accounting for the physical routing and operational overheads of large-scale fault-tolerant architectures.

Other qLDPC codes have been shown to achieve the stronger scaling, with both the number of logical qubits and the distance growing proportionally to the physical-qubit count \cite{panteleev2022asymptotically,LeverrierZemor2022}. Such families are called asymptotically good, but usually feature high-weight parity checks \cite{lin2022good}.
Weight-reduction methods can lower pariity-check weights and qubit degrees while preserving asymptotic goodness, at the cost of additional physical qubits~\cite{WangEtAl2026,HastingsWeightReduction}.
Nevertheless, their practical value depends on the resource overheads associated with syndrome extraction circuits, physical qubit routing, and the execution of fault-tolerant logical operations.

On the other hand, hyperbolic surface codes preserve the surface-based description of checks and logical operators, while offering a compelling combination of encoding efficiency, growing distance, and geometric structure \cite{freedman2002z2, albuquerque2009topological, BreuckmannTerhal2016}. They are defined on closed higher-genus Riemann surfaces whose number of handles grows with the code size, allowing $k$ to grow proportionally to \(n\). For surface codes with bounded face sizes and vertex degrees, Delfosse established the upper bound
\[
\frac{kd^2}{n}\leq C(\log k)^2,
\]
where \(C\) depends on the local geometry \cite{Delfosse2013}. This permits the code's efficiency $kd^2/n$ to grow with code size beyond the constant bound imposed by Euclidean locality. The bound alone does not guarantee that a particular construction realizes this growth. A central question, that we address in this work, is therefore how closely explicit finite surface codes can approach this scaling while retaining low-weight checks and manageable connectivity. 

Translating this theoretical potential into a viable superconducting architecture requires bridging abstract hyperbolic geometry with explicit stabilizer codes and physical hardware constraints. In particular, realizing hyperbolic surface codes introduces four fundamental hurdles: (i) systematically constructing logical operators that satisfy the canonical Pauli commutation relations, (ii) embedding non-Euclidean negative curvature into a planar two-dimensional superconducting layout, (iii) optimizing periodic identifications to obtain code families that leverage the optimal efficiency scaling \cite{Delfosse2013}, and (iv) mitigating the extensive non-local routing required by the periodic lattice connectivity.
Our earlier work addressed the first challenge by establishing a systematic framework for identifying parity checks and logical operators for hyperbolic codes \cite{MahmoudAliRayan2026}, while our subsequent work addressed the second by engineering capacitive couplings between superconducting resonators to emulate the Poincaré metric \cite{XuEtAl2025}. While this demonstration provided a viable physical platform for open-boundary hyperbolic connectivity, realizing these codes as fault-tolerant devices requires scalable routing protocols to implement long-range connectivity. In this work, we address the third and fourth challenges by demonstrating that substantial efficiency gains emerge from optimizing the compactification of the hyperbolic surface, in addition to introducing a topology-aware compiler to modularize hyperbolic codes, while systematically assessing the routing overhead in both monolithic and modular architectures.

Specifically, we perform an exhaustive search over self-dual prime-field  $\{p,p\}$ tessellations for $p \in \{5,6,7,8\}$, uncovering code efficiency of $kd^2/n \simeq 33.34$ in a $[[51330, 17112, 10]]$ code that harbors over $17,000$ logical qubits. We demonstrate that tailoring the periodic boundary conditions can double the code distance at fixed qubit count, encoding rate, and check weight. Furthermore, selected $\{5,5\}$ and $\{7,7\}$ code families exhibit finite-size scaling consistent with the optimal code-efficiency scaling allowed by Delfosse's bound. 
To facilitate hardware implementation, we introduce a topology-aware modular partitioning algorithm that preserves the code structure and satisfies the prescribed capacity and geometric constraints.
Its reference layouts reduce all five communication metrics relative to the tested load-feasible spectral-partitioning assignments.
Finally, via circuit-level Monte Carlo simulations, we quantify threshold degradation under noisier long-range gates across both monolithic and modular architectures (summarized in Fig.~\ref{fig1:overview}). We envision that, alongside our preceding results, this work establishes a concrete architectural blueprint toward scalable, high-rate fault-tolerant quantum memories based on hyperbolic surface codes.

Section~\ref{sec:construction} presents the code construction, search, and distance calculations. Section~\ref{sec:family} examines finite-size efficiency scaling. Sections~\ref{sec:architectures} and~\ref{sec:compiler} address the physical-connectivity and introduces our modular partitioning algorithm, respectively. Section~\ref{sec:circuit} reports memory performance, while Sec.~\ref{sec:discussion} concludes with the implications and explores future directions and requirements for practical fault-tolerant architectures based on hyperbolic surface codes.

