\section{Code construction, exhaustive search, and distance calculation}
\label{sec:construction}

A regular hyperbolic tiling specifies the local structure of the code, while boundary identifications compactify a finite region into a closed surface and determine its global connectivity. For a \(\{p,p\}\) tiling, we utilize the orientation-preserving triangle group
\begin{equation}
\Delta_p=\langle x,y\mid x^p=y^p=(xy)^2=1\rangle,
\label{eq:triangle}
\end{equation}
to define the underlying hyperbolic tiling. A finite quotient
\begin{equation}
Q=\Delta_p/\Gamma_{\mathrm{PBC}}
\end{equation}
then specifies the periodic identifications. Faces, vertices, and edges are represented by cosets of the subgroups \(\langle x\rangle\), \(\langle y\rangle\), and \(\langle xy\rangle\), respectively \cite{Siran2001,MahmoudAliRayan2026}.

Data qubits occupy the edges, while each face defines a \(Z\) check on its boundary edges, and each vertex defines an \(X\) check on its incident edges. These checks commute because a face boundary meets each vertex an even number of times. For a closed, connected, orientable surface of genus \(g\), the code encodes \(k=2g\) logical qubits.

The local tiling structure fixes several code parameters before the periodic identifications are chosen. Writing \(F\), \(V\), and \(E\) for the numbers of faces, vertices, and edges, respectively, gives
\begin{equation}
pF=pV=2E,\qquad n=E.
\end{equation}
Euler's relation then yields
\begin{equation}
\frac{k}{n}=\frac{p-4}{p}+\frac{2}{n}.
\label{eq:rate}
\end{equation}
Both check types have weight \(p\), and each data qubit participates in two checks of each type. Assigning one ancilla qubit to every check requires \(V+F=4n/p\) ancilla qubits and \(4n\) data--ancilla interactions per syndrome round.

At fixed tiling and quotient-group order, altering the periodic identifications leaves these parameters unchanged but can alter the shortest logical operators. Therefore, periodic identifications provide a means of improving the code distance at fixed physical-qubit count, encoding rate, and check weight.

We restrict the quotient group to $Q \cong \operatorname{PSL}(2,q)$, where $q$ is prime. This group comprises $2 \times 2$ determinant-one matrices over the finite field $\mathbb{F}_q$, taken modulo its center. For odd $q$, the group order is $|Q| = q(q^2-1)/2$. Because the data qubits of the code reside on the edges of the tessellation—which correspond algebraically to the cosets of the order-two subgroup $\langle xy \rangle$—the total number of data qubits is exactly $n = |Q|/2$. 
Here, $q$ determines the quotient-group order and hence the code size, while $p$ determines the local tiling; admissible pairs must satisfy the generator conditions below.

To enforce the lattice's self-duality, we introduce a projective involution $J \in \operatorname{PGL}(2,q)$ and constrain the generators such that $y = JxJ^{-1}$. We consider both split and non-split involutions, characterized respectively by having two or zero fixed points on the projective line over $\mathbb{F}_q$. Valid generating pairs $(x,y)$ must satisfy the triangle-group relations $\operatorname{ord}(x) = \operatorname{ord}(y) = p$ and $\operatorname{ord}(xy) = 2$, while collectively generating the full quotient group $Q$. By construction, conjugation by $J$ swaps $x$ and $y$, structurally embedding a strict face--vertex symmetry into the resulting code.

To remove redundancies under the chosen algebraic equivalence, we group generator pairs into orbits under conjugation by the centralizer of $J$ in $\operatorname{PGL}(2,q)$ (the subgroup of elements that commute with $J$).
We retain only a single representative from each orbit, and the class counts reported below refer to this specific equivalence relation.
We perform an exhaustive enumeration of this algebraic construction
for tiling parameters $p\in\{5,6,7,8\}$ across all 17 primes $q<60$, giving 68 tiling--field pairs. Recording both swap labels for each
pair gives 136 cases, of which 115 contain no candidate generators.
The remaining 21 cases produce 2,304 candidate generator pairs,
which form 38 centralizer classes.
Each retained class undergoes verification to ensure it satisfies the triangle-group relations, generates the correct group order, and forms a valid, closed, orientable, and fully connected cellulation. 
The exhaustive claim applies specifically to regular, closed $\{p,p\}$ hyperbolic codes that admit the specified projective face--vertex swap and possess prime-field $\operatorname{PSL}(2,q)$ quotient groups.

For these hyperbolic surface codes, logical operators correspond to non-trivial homological cycles on the code manifold: $Z$-type logical operators map to cycles in the primal graph, while $X$-type logical operators map to cycles in the dual graph. A cycle is considered logically trivial (a stabilizer) if its edge vector can be expressed as a linear combination of face boundaries over $\mathbb{F}_2$. The homological code distance is dictated by the shortest non-trivial cycle, commonly referred to as the systole of the surface, such that $d = \min(d_X, d_Z)$. Because the primal and dual graphs can theoretically exhibit different systole lengths, the code distance is computed independently to determine both $d_X$ and $d_Z$.

To systematically evaluate and compare these codes, we adopt the efficiency metric 
\begin{equation}
\eta=\frac{kd^2}{n}.\label{eq:efficiency}
\end{equation}
Table~\ref{tab:optimal-codes} presents the maximum-efficiency representative for each tiling parameter derived from the exhaustive search. The global optimum within this parameter space is achieved by the \(\{6,6\}\) code \([[51330,17112,10]]\), which exhibits a remarkable encoding efficiency of \(\eta\simeq33.34\).

