Although the \(\{8,8\}\) construction boasts the highest asymptotic rate, the superior distance of the optimal \(\{6,6\}\) code ultimately yields a higher overall efficiency. 
Conversely, the \(\{5,5\}\) construction achieves a maximum distance of 12 using weight-five checks, albeit bounded by a lower asymptotic rate of one fifth.

Figure~\ref{fig:systematic-search} isolates the impact of periodic identifications by comparing distinct generating maps that share identical tiling parameters, quotient groups, physical-qubit counts, check-weights and encoding rates. For instance, within the \(\{7,7\}\) construction at \(q=41\), different identifications yield exact distances of 4, 6, and 8. Selecting the distance-eight map over its distance-four counterpart doubles the code distance, thereby quadrupling the efficiency \(\eta\). Crucially, this substantial enhancement is achieved entirely through the optimization of periodic boundary conditions; it incurs no additional overhead in data-qubit count, hardware check weight, or the number of two-qubit interactions required per syndrome extraction round.

