To place these results in context, we compare our static constructions against the recently developed hyperbolic and semi-hyperbolic Floquet codes~\cite{HiggottBreuckmann2024}. While the static surface codes studied here require the direct measurement of fixed multi-qubit stabilizer generators, Floquet codes dynamically maintain the code space through a periodic schedule of two-qubit measurements. Nevertheless, the effective logical parameters of both paradigms are fundamentally governed by the topology of the underlying hyperbolic manifold, allowing for a direct comparison of their code-parameter efficiencies. Here, $d$ represents the homological distance, while for the Floquet constructions, $d$ denotes the embedded homological distance defined in Ref.~\cite{HiggottBreuckmann2024}.

Among the hyperbolic Floquet instances reported in that work, the maximum code efficiency is $\eta \simeq 5.01$, realized by a $[[19200,962,10]]$ code~\cite{HiggottBreuckmann2024}. In contrast, within our enumerated regular self-dual $\operatorname{PSL}(2,q)$ constructions for prime $q < 60$, the maximum efficiency is attained by the $\{6,6\}$ code $[[51330,17112,10]]$. This construction achieves an efficiency of $\eta \simeq 33.34$, representing a roughly $\times 6.65$ improvement over the Floquet benchmark. 
Our optimal $\{7,7\}$ code yields an efficiency of $\eta \simeq 27.44$. This exceeds the baseline Floquet efficiency by a factor of approximately $5.48$ while utilizing roughly $10.3\%$ fewer data qubits than the $[[19200,962,10]]$ benchmark. The conventional Euclidean toric code leverages $\eta=1$; therefore, our best code instance achieves $\times33.34$ improvement over the toric code while retaining the surface-based geometric description.

\section{Finite-size efficiency scaling}
\label{sec:family}

The exhaustive search allows us to examine how code efficiency grows with the number of logical qubits. Delfosse's bound limits \(\eta=kd^2/n=\mathcal{O}((\log k)^2)\) for surface codes with bounded local geometry \cite{Delfosse2013}. We therefore compare the computed efficiencies with \((\log k)^2\) over the available finite sequences.

For each fixed tiling, we select the smallest physical-qubit count at which each new maximum distance is attained within the exhaustive search. This selection favors increasing protection without retaining larger codes that offer no distance gain.
The resulting \(\{5,5\}\) and \(\{7,7\}\) sequences are listed in Table~\ref{tab:scaling-sequences}. The \(\{5,5\}\) sequence uses weight-five checks and has rate
\begin{equation}
\frac{k}{n}=\frac{1}{5}+\frac{2}{n},
\end{equation}
while the \(\{7,7\}\) sequence uses weight-seven checks and has rate
\begin{equation}
\frac{k}{n}=\frac{3}{7}+\frac{2}{n}.
\end{equation}
The weight-five and weight-seven sequences span more than three and two orders of magnitude in logical-qubit count, respectively.

