These results establish explicit finite constructions with favorable efficiency growth at fixed check weight. Their immediate significance is that the observed advantage persists across large code instances required for large-scale fault-tolerant quantum computing. 
The following sections examine interaction layouts and circuit-level memory performance for these codes.

\section{From closed topology to physical connectivity}
\label{sec:architectures}
The same periodic identifications that create a high-rate code also determine long-range interactions that must be supplied by the hardware. Engineered couplings can mimic local connectivity of the hyperbolic metric, as demonstrated in the superconducting-resonator framework of Ref.~\cite{XuEtAl2025}. However, the long-range interactions augment chip design efforts with additional engineering challenges, such as devising routing protocols that minimize crosstalk between qubits.

Here we study two interaction layouts motivated by superconducting hardware: a modular processor that distributes connected planar regions across separate chips and uses inter-module gates to complete the checks, and a monolithic processor that places the code on one planar chip and supplies selected non-local gates through long-range routing channels. 
Existing work on networked error correction, modular qLDPC codes, and distributed hyperbolic Floquet memories motivates the modular layout~\cite{NickersonEtAl2014,StrikisBerent2023,SutcliffeEtAl2025,RametteEtAl2024}, alongside demonstrations of reconfigurable routers and gates between separate superconducting modules~\cite{WuEtAl2024,SongEtAl2025}. Cavity buses and long-range superconducting coupling schemes provide relevant building blocks for the monolithic layout~\cite{majer2007coupling,marxer2023long,xiong2025scalable,deng2025long}.

For both architectures, the communication overhead is determined by the two-qubit entangling gates executed between data and ancilla qubits. Because an edge connecting distant boundary vertices in the primal lattice does not generally correspond to a single non-local operation, we explicitly map both qubit species and enumerate the resulting long-range data--ancilla interactions. In the modular architecture, an interaction is considered remote if the participating qubits reside in distinct modules. In the monolithic architecture, an interaction is classified as long-range if realizing qubit adjacency requires routing across periodic boundary conditions.

In our circuit simulations, each required remote interaction is modeled as a two-qubit $\mathrm{CNOT}$ gate subject to an elevated error probability. 
In particular, a local $\mathrm{CNOT}$ gate operates with a baseline error probability $p$, whereas a remote gate incurs a proportional penalty, operating with an elevated error probability $\alpha p$ for some multiplier $\alpha \geq 1$.
This effective error model isolates the operational impact of remote gate infidelity under a fixed schedule, abstracting away physical link lengths and the lower-level overheads of routing buses, communication ports, or entanglement-generation protocols.

\section{Topology-aware modular compilation}
\label{sec:compiler}

In this section, we introduce a topology-aware compilation algorithm designed to partition closed hyperbolic surface codes into planar modules of bounded size. The primary objective is to minimize inter-module communication overhead while satisfying explicit geometric and hardware-capacity constraints.

The syndrome extraction circuit is modeled as a bipartite interaction graph, commonly referred to as a Tanner graph~\cite{tanner1981recursive}. The vertex set comprises the data qubits alongside one ancilla qubit for every face and vertex check; the edges represent the data--ancilla interactions. Consequently, for a regular \(\{p,p\}\) code, the total number of physical qubits (data plus ancillas) is \(n_{\mathrm{total}}=n(1+4/p)\). 

Given a module assignment mapping \(m(\cdot)\), we quantify the communication overhead using two primary metrics:
\begin{align}
R&=\bigl|\{(c,u):u\in S_c,\ m(c)\ne m(u)\}\bigr|,\label{eq:routing-R}\\
K&=\sum_c\left(\left|\{m(c)\}\cup\{m(u):u\in S_c\}\right|-1\right),\label{eq:routing-K}
\end{align}
where \(S_c\) denotes the data-qubit support of check \(c\). Here, \(R\) measures the total number of remote (inter-module) interactions per complete syndrome extraction round, while \(K\) quantifies the aggregate distribution of checks across multiple modules. Specifically, a check confined to a single module contributes zero to \(K\), whereas a check spanning three modules contributes two. The latter serves as an agnostic measure of check fragmentation, independent of the underlying physical routing protocol.

We also report three other communication metrics. The \textit{number of communicating module pairs} counts distinct pairs of modules connected by at least one data--ancilla interaction.
The \textit{maximum number of communication partners} is the largest number of distinct modules with which any one module interacts.
The \textit{maximum remote-interaction count} is the largest number of inter-module CNOTs involving any module during one syndrome-extraction round.
Each inter-module CNOT contributes one to the load of each participating module.

To model realistic hardware constraints, we impose a strict upper bound of \(B_{\max}=80\) qubits per module. The total number of modules $M$ is given by:
\begin{equation}
M=\left\lceil\frac{n_{\mathrm{total}}}{80}\right\rceil,\quad
\bar B=\frac{n_{\mathrm{total}}}{M},\quad
B_{\min}=\lfloor0.8\bar B\rfloor.
\label{eq:routing-load}
\end{equation}
Every module must encapsulate between \(B_{\min}\) and 80 qubits (including both data and ancillas). 
These capacity bounds are enforced during the qubit-placement phase and verified upon completion.
Furthermore, the local interaction graph of each module must remain both connected and planar. Topologically, the union of faces constituting a module must form a strict topological disk---a connected region featuring a single boundary loop and devoid of any handles.

Algorithm~\ref{alg:topology-compiler} details the topology-aware protocol, executed in three sequential phases: face partitioning, physical qubit assignment, and validation. We emphasize that this compiler acts solely as a physical routing map; it preserves the code structure as well as every data qubit and parity check, leaving the logical code parameters \([[n,k,d]]\) unaltered.

\begin{algorithm}[H]
\caption{Topology-aware modular compilation}
\label{alg:topology-compiler}
\small
\algrenewcommand\algorithmicrequire{\textbf{Input:}}
\algrenewcommand\algorithmicensure{\textbf{Output:}}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\Require $\{p,p\}$ hyperbolic surface code; bounds \(M, B_{\min}, B_{\max}\) from Eq.~\eqref{eq:routing-load}; maximum number of attempts \(T\).
\Ensure A partition into \(M\) face regions, a module assignment for every data and ancilla qubit, and the five communication metrics defined earlier. 
The assignment is accepted only if it satisfies all capacity and geometric constraints; otherwise, the run terminates as a failure.
\Statex \textbf{Step 1: Partition the faces}
\For{up to $T$ attempts}
    \State Initialize regions by selecting a random starting face, iteratively adding $M-1$ subsequent faces that maximize the dual-graph distance from the current set.
    \State Grow regions by adding at most one adjacent face per region, prioritizing smaller regions and candidates sharing more edges with the region; stop when complete or stalled.
    \State Reject any face addition that violates the topological disk condition or exceeds the maximum face limit \(f_{\max}\).
    \If{all faces are successfully assigned and every region contains at least \(f_{\min}\) faces}
        \State Retain the current face partition and \textbf{break}.
    \EndIf
\EndFor
\State \textbf{if} no valid face partition is found, \textbf{return} failure.
\Statex \textbf{Step 2: Assign data and ancilla qubits}
\State Data qubits whose two incident faces lie on one region are assigned to that region, while data qubits whose incident faces lie on two regions are assigned heuristically to either incident region.
\State Iteratively optimize data-qubit assignments by minimizing the cost function $\widetilde{C}$ in Eq.~\eqref{eq:routing-surrogate}. Terminate after 20 passes or when no accepted moves remain.
\State Assign each face-check ancilla to its corresponding face region.
\State Assign each vertex-check ancilla to a module containing at least one of its support qubits, minimizing remote interactions subject to the module capacity bounds \(B_{\min}\leq B_j\leq B_{\max}\).
\Statex \textbf{Step 3: Validate and measure}
\State \textbf{if} assignment fails to satisfy any capacity or geometric constraints \textbf{return} failure.
\State Compute the five communication metrics: \(R\), \(K\), the number of communicating module pairs, the maximum number of communication partners, and the maximum remote-interaction count.
\State \Return the face regions, qubit assignments, and communication metrics.
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

The partitioning phase operates on the dual graph of the lattice, where vertices represent the faces of the primal lattice and edges connect adjacent faces. For a lattice with \(F\) total faces, the permissible number of faces per module is bounded by:
\begin{equation}
f_{\min}=\max\left\{2,\lfloor0.8F/M\rfloor\right\},\quad
f_{\max}=\lceil1.2F/M\rceil.
\end{equation}

With the face regions fixed, we optimize the permitted
data-qubit assignments using the Tanner graph.
Data-qubit optimization utilizes a cost function, \(\widetilde C\), formulated as:
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
\widetilde C={}&\sum_c\bigl[10\bigl(|S_c|-\max_b t_{cb}\bigr)
+|\{b:t_{cb}>0\}|-1\bigr]\\
&+\sum_b[\max(0,\ell_b-L)]^2,
\end{aligned}
\label{eq:routing-surrogate}
\end{equation}
where \(t_{cb}\) denotes the number of data qubits belonging to check \(c\) that currently reside in module \(b\), \(\ell_b\) is the data-qubit load of module \(b\), and \(L=\min\{80,\lceil n/M\rceil+2\}\) is the target data-qubit capacity. 
A proposed data-qubit reassignment is accepted if it decreases
\(\widetilde C\), or leaves \(\widetilde C\) unchanged while decreasing
the load-penalty term.

Face-check ancillas are assigned to their corresponding face regions. The remaining vertex-check ancillas are assigned jointly to minimize remote interactions subject to module-capacity bounds, with each ancilla assigned to a module containing at least one of its support qubits. If module $j$ contains $b_j$ data qubits and face-check ancillas, it can receive between $\max(0,B_{\min}-b_j)$ and $B_{\max}-b_j$ vertex-check ancillas. The five communication metrics, described earlier, are evaluated only after all qubits have been placed.

To evaluate the compilation protocol, we benchmark one code for each check weight \(p \in \{5,6,7,8\}\). These instances are precisely the maximum-efficiency codes (\(kd^2/n\)) identified in the exhaustive search and presented in Table~\ref{tab:optimal-codes}. Consequently, all four instances are selected systematically by efficiency, independent of their innate routing performance. Notably, the \(\{5,5\}\) benchmark is the \([[51330,10268,12]]\) code and its reference module assignment is precisely the one utilized in the subsequent modular circuit simulations presented in Sec.~\ref{sec:circuit}. The reported reference assignments represent the initial recorded run for each code; we do not post-select the best communication cost across multiple trials.

We benchmark our topology-aware protocol against two standard graph-partitioning compilers. The first is recursive spectral bisection (RSB) applied to the dual graph, followed by the identical face-to-qubit assignment conversion used in our protocol. The second is Mt-KaHyPar~\cite{GottesbuerenEtAl2023}, applied directly to the Tanner graph to explicitly minimize the remote interaction count \(R\), utilizing its highest-quality single-thread configuration. Spectral partitioning is a well-established baseline, having been employed in previous distributed hyperbolic-code studies~\cite{SutcliffeEtAl2025,Galimova2026}. In our benchmarking, all methods enforce the same module count \(M\) and upper capacity of 80 qubits. During ancilla placement, RSB enforces the same lower capacity bound, whereas Mt-KaHyPar enforces only the upper bound natively, with lower bounds verified \textit{post hoc}. Crucially, neither baseline enforces the geometric constraints required for modular planarity; these topological properties are evaluated independently of the communication cost.

