\section{Memory performance with noisier long-range operations}
\label{sec:circuit}

The hardware assignments described in Secs.~\ref{sec:architectures} and~\ref{sec:compiler} determine which two-qubit operations require nonlocal interactions. We now
evaluate the sensitivity of hyperbolic surface code quantum memories to errors in these operations,
using the largest four members of the closed $\{5,5\}$ code family presented in
Table~\ref{tab:scaling-sequences}.
For each instance, we compare two fixed assignments of nonlocal gates: an
effective-modular implementation based on our topology-aware partitioning algorithm described in Sec.~\ref{sec:compiler} and
a monolithic implementation with native long-range connections. The hyperbolic surface codes, as well as their selected logical operator, ideal extraction circuit, and noise probabilities are otherwise held fixed. 
This isolates the effect of the long-range interactions  and their associated gate-error penalties within a common circuit-level noise model.

Each experiment performs \(d\) consecutive syndrome-extraction
rounds, where \(d\) is the homological code distance. For the simulated
\(\{5,5\}\) codes, each check involves five data qubits and requires
five CNOTs between its ancilla and those qubits. In each round,
the ancillas are prepared, the CNOTs are applied in five parallel
layers for the vertex checks followed by five for the face checks,
and the ancillas are measured. Within each layer, no qubit
participates in more than one CNOT. Since each data qubit
participates in two vertex checks and two face checks, a complete
round contains \(4n\) CNOTs, where \(n\) is the number of data qubits.
We perform $Z$-memory experiments and measure the probability $p_L$ that decoding fails to recover one selected logical $Z$ operator after the complete experiment. 
We use a stochastic circuit-level noise model inspired by
SI1000, designed to be representative of the underlying error probabilities in contemporary superconducting quantum chips~\cite{GidneyEtAl2021SI1000}. A local CNOT is followed by two-qubit depolarizing noise with probability $p$. To account for the degraded fidelity of nonlocal gates, a nonlocal CNOT has error probability $\alpha p$, with $\alpha\in\{1,2,3\}$. Reset and measurement errors occur with probabilities $2p$ and $5p$, respectively. Idle qubits experience depolarizing noise with probability $p/10$ during ordinary gate layers and $2p$ while waiting through reset or measurement layers.

We use Stim to generate the circuits and the detector error
models~\cite{Gidney2021}, while decoding is done using correlated minimum-weight perfect matching implemented in PyMatching~\cite{Higgott2022}. 
For the selected logical $Z$-memory circuits, Stim's graphlike-error search gives circuit-fault distances $D=4,5,7,8$, compared with homological distances $d=6,8,10,12$.
These reduced distances characterize fault propagation through the specified measurement schedule; they do not alter the homological code parameters used in Secs.~\ref{sec:construction} and~\ref{sec:family}. In particular, a different noise model or measurement schedule might result in a different circuit-fault distance, while the homological distance is agnostic to the underlying error model and only depends on the code's structure.

For the modular implementation, we use the topology-aware disk
partitioning algorithm outlined in Sec.~\ref{sec:compiler}. A CNOT is nonlocal precisely when its data and ancilla
qubits belong to different modules. For the largest instance, 51,330 data
qubits and 41,064 check ancillas occupy 1,155 modules; 69,021 of the
205,320 CNOTs in each round are nonlocal. The inter-module
fraction remains close to one-third across the four sizes.

For the monolithic implementation, we represent the finite
\(\{5,5\}\) code in the Poincaré disk model. Vertex-check ancillas are placed at the lattice vertices, face-check ancillas at the geometric
centres of faces, and data qubits at edge midpoints.
A CNOT is classified as local when it connects an ancilla to a data qubit on an incident edge in this representation. 
A CNOT is classified as long-range when its data and ancilla qubits positions are connected through a periodic identification rather than a local incidence.

