\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:intro}

In this paper, we consider two problems: \emph{Local Leakage Resilience} (LLR) of Shamir secret sharing; and \emph{Optimal Polynomial Intersection} (OPI).  While seemingly unrelated at first, these two problems were connected by \cite{us}.  Leveraging this connection, we obtain improvements on both problems.  Our main results, described in more detail below, include improved rate thresholds for LLR for Shamir, past the so-called ``rate-$1/2$ barrier''; and improved results (both existential and algorithmic) for OPI. 

\subsection{Local Leakage Resilience}
Local Leakage Resilience is a property of a secret sharing scheme.  We focus on \emph{Shamir secret sharing.}  In Shamir secret sharing, a secret $s \in \F_p$ is shared among $n$ parties as follows: We choose a uniformly random polynomial $P$ of degree at most $k$ so that $P(0) = s$.  Then we share $P(\alpha_i)$ with party $i$, where $\alpha_1, \ldots, \alpha_n$ are non-zero, distinct evaluation points.  This scheme has the property that any $k+1$ of the parties together can recover the secret $s$, while any $k$ of them together learn nothing about it. We parameterize a sharing polynomial as $P(X)=s+XQ(X)$ with $\deg Q<k$. 

Informally, we say that a secret sharing scheme is ($b$-bit) \emph{Local Leakage Resilient} (LLR) if, when each of the parties may leak up to $b$ bits of their shares, the secret is still secure.  Formally, we have the following definition.

\begin{definition}[Local Leakage Resilience for Shamir's Secret-Sharing Scheme]
\label{def:llr}
Fix nonzero evaluation points $\alpha_1, \ldots, \alpha_n \in \F_p^{\times}$ and let $C \subset \F_p^n$ be the linear code\footnote{A \emph{linear code} is just an $\F_p$-linear subspace of $\F_p^n$.}
\begin{equation}
 \label{eq:secret-zero-code}
 C:= \{(\alpha_i Q(\alpha_i))_{i=1}^n \,:\, \deg(Q) < k \}.
\end{equation}
 For $v \in \F_p^n$, let $L_v$ denote the distribution of the \emph{transcript} $(\ell_1(c_1), \ldots, \ell_n(c_n))$, when $c$ is chosen uniformly at random from the coset $v + C$.
 We say that (the secret-sharing scheme defined by) $C$ is \emph{$b$-bit Local Leakage Resilient (LLR)} if, for any \emph{leakage maps} $\ell_i:\F_p \to \{0,1\}^b$, and for any pair of secrets $s,s' \in \F_p$, 
 \[ \TV(L_{s\one}, L_{s'\one}) \leq e^{-\Omega(n)}.\]
\end{definition}
While LLR makes sense for any secret-sharing scheme, we specialize to Shamir's scheme.  LLR for Shamir's scheme has received special attention lately, first because Shamir's scheme is a fundamental secret sharing scheme, and second because it is known~\cite{gw,tyb18} that over \emph{extension fields}, Shamir's scheme is \emph{not} locally leakage resilient.  Surprisingly, \cite{bdir_conference} (who also first explicitly introduced the notion of LLR) showed that over \emph{prime} fields, in fact Shamir's scheme is $1$-bit LLR provided that $R := k/n$ satisfies $R \geq 0.92$.  This state of affairs leads to the following question:
\begin{question}\label{q:llr}
What is the smallest rate $R^*$ so that, for all $R > R^*$, Shamir's scheme over a prime field $\F_p$ with $k/n = R$ is one-bit LLR?
\end{question}
 After a long line of work~\cite{bdir, mpsw21,mnpw22,kk23,n24,k24}, the best answer to \cref{q:llr} prior to this paper was due to \cite{k24}, and states that one can take $R^* \leq 0.668$.  Moreover, the work \cite{n24} identified a natural barrier at $R^* = 1/2$ (see \cref{sec:related_LLR}), and asked if it was possible to obtain $R^* < 1/2$.

\paragraph{Our results for local leakage resilience.} We improve the state-of-the art on \cref{q:llr}, and in particular we break the ``1/2-barrier'' for one-bit LLR.  More precisely, we prove the following theorem.

\begin{restatable}[Local Leakage Resilience of Shamir Schemes]{theorem}{mainllr}
\label{thm:main-llr}
Every Shamir secret sharing scheme with rate $R$ over a prime field is
\begin{enumerate}
    \item $b$-bit locally leakage resilient for every fixed $b$ and every fixed $R>1/2$;
    \item one-bit locally leakage resilient whenever
    $R>1/2-\delta$, for some absolute constant $\delta >0$.
\end{enumerate}
\end{restatable}
This improves on the best previous result of \cite{k24} that required $R > 0.668$, and is the first result to break the $1/2$ barrier, for $1$-bit worst-case leakage functions.  It is also the first result to even approach $R = 1/2$ for any $b \geq 1$ for $b$-bit worst-case leakage functions.

\begin{remark}
    We emphasize that \cref{thm:main-llr} applies for \emph{arbitrary} leakage functions (in particular, worst-case and not necessarily ``balanced,'' meaning that $\ell_i^{-1}(1)$ may have any size between $0$ and $p$).  However, it still improves on the best known result in the balanced case (when $|\ell_i^{-1}(1)| \sim p/2$ for all $i$).  This result was due to \cite{kk23}, and established $R^* \leq 0.58$ in the balanced case.
    In fact, as part of our proof, we show the balanced case is in fact the \emph{hardest}, in the sense that the sufficient rate $R^*$ threshold in the balanced case hold for arbitrary biases.
\end{remark}

\subsection{Optimal Polynomial Intersection}

The second problem we study is \emph{Optimal Polynomial Intersection} (OPI).  OPI is a discrete optimization problem about low-degree polynomials over finite fields.  Given \emph{input lists} $S_1, \ldots, S_n \subset \F_p$ and distinct evaluation points $\alpha_1, \ldots, \alpha_n \in \F_p$, the goal is to find a polynomial $Q(X) \in \F_p[X]$ of degree less than $k$ so that $Q(\alpha_i) \in S_i$ for as many $i$ as possible.  Formally, we have the following definition.

\begin{definition}[Optimal Polynomial Intersection]
\label{def:opi}
Fix distinct evaluation points $\alpha_1,\ldots,\alpha_n\in\F_p$ and nonempty input lists $S_1,\ldots,S_n\subset\F_p$. The OPI problem asks for a polynomial $Q \in \F_p[X]$ with $\deg(Q) < k$ that maximizes the \emph{satisfaction ratio}
\[
s(Q):=\frac1n\abs{\left\{i:Q(\alpha_i)\in S_i\right\}}.
\]
We will often refer to $R := k/n$ as the \emph{rate} of the OPI instance.\footnote{We use this terminology as $R$ is the rate of the corresponding Reed--Solomon code.}
\end{definition}
OPI can be viewed as the problem of finding a vector in or near the set $C \cap \mathbf{S}$, where $\mathbf{S} = S_1 \times \cdots \times S_n$\footnote{Sometimes we abuse notation and refer by $\mbf{S}$ to both $(S_1, \dots, S_n)$ as well as $S_1\times\dots\times S_n$.}
and $C$ is the \emph{Reed--Solomon code}
\[
C := \{ (Q(\alpha_1), \ldots, Q(\alpha_n)) : Q \in \F_p[X], \deg(Q) < k \}.
\]
If $\rho_i = |S_i|/p$ is the density of $S_i$, and if the sets $S_i$ were random, we would expect $|C \cap \mathbf{S}| \approx |C| \prod_{i=1}^n \rho_i$.  The OPI problem above asks us to find an element in $C \cap \mathbf{S}$ if there is one; but at an existential level we may also wonder such an element exists for worst-case $S_i$, and if so how many.  In particular, is the number of such elements close to what we would expect for random lists?  This leads to the following definition, inspired by \cite{dlmnrr}, which studied similar discrepancy notions for random linear codes.
\begin{definition}[Near-optimal OPI Discrepancy]\label{def:disc}
Let $C\subset\F_p^n$, and fix $S_1, \ldots, S_n \subset \F_p$. Let $\rho_i:=|S_i|/p$ and let $\mbf{S}:=S_1\times\cdots\times S_n$. We say that $C$ has \emph{near-optimal OPI discrepancy} for input lists $S_1,\ldots,S_n$ if
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:def-perfect-opi}
\abs{\frac{|C\cap \mbf{S}|}{|C|\prod_{i=1}^n\rho_i}-1}
\le e^{-\Omega(n)}.
\end{equation}
\end{definition}

OPI is related to many problems that have been studied in different areas over the years.  For example, when the lists $S_i$ are relatively small, the question is related to \emph{list-recovery} of Reed-Solomon codes~\cite{gs,GR06,LP20,lms25,LS25,bcdz,bcpzz,gj26}; and in other parameter regimes, similar problems have been proposed as cryptographic hardness assumptions~\cite{np99,bn00,NP06}.  Most recently, OPI has gained prominence as a candidate for quantum supremacy.  The work \cite{dqi} gave a quantum algorithm, \emph{Decoded Quantum Interferometry} (DQI), which can find solutions to OPI with satisfaction ratio at least $\SCL_\rho(R/2)-o(1)$, where $R = k/n$ and where $\SCL$ is the \emph{semicircle law}
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:semicircle-law}
\SCL_\rho(\alpha):=
\begin{cases}
\left(\sqrt{\rho(1-\alpha)}+\sqrt{(1-\rho)\alpha}\right)^2 &\text{if }0\le \alpha\le1-\rho\\
1 &\text{if } \alpha\ge 1-\rho
\end{cases}
\end{equation}
In contrast, the best known classical algorithm can only find solutions with satisfaction ratio about $\rho + R(1 - \rho)$, which is smaller.  This leads to the following question.
\begin{question}\label{q:OPI}
For a fixed rate $R = k/n$ and fixed $\rho \in (0,1)$, what is the largest  $s^* \in (0,1]$ so that every OPI instance of rate $R$ with $|S_i|\ge \rho p$ for all $i$ admits a solution $Q \in \F_p[X]$ with satisfaction ratio $s(Q) \geq s^*$?  What is the largest fraction $s^*_{\mathrm{alg}}  \in (0,1]$ so that a solution $Q \in \F_p[X]$ with $s(Q) \geq s^*_{\mathrm{alg}}$ can be found efficiently with a quantum algorithm?
\end{question}
This question (both the existential and algorithmic versions) was first addressed by \cite{dqi}, who as mentioned above showed that $s^*, s^*_{\mathrm{alg}} \geq \SCL_\rho(R/2)$.  
Since then, several works~\cite{chailloux1,chailloux2,us,hy,sjo} have improved on this result, both existential and algorithmic. These improvements are depicted in \cref{fig:opi-picture}, and discussed more in \cref{sec:related_OPI}.  In particular, the best previous algorithmic results were limited at $s^*_{\mathrm{alg}} \leq \SCL_\rho(\alpha_J(R))$, where $\alpha_J(R) = 1 - \sqrt{1 - R}$ is the \emph{Johnson radius}; and for most of the range $R \in (0,1)$, this was not for worst-case input lists.\footnote{The works \cite{chailloux1,chailloux2} assume that the input lists $S_i$ are translated by i.i.d. random additive shifts $v_i \in \F_p$.}  In the balanced case, perfect solutions could be found efficiently only for $R \geq 0.75$.
 As we will see next, our results yield an algorithmic improvement all the way to $s^*_{\mathrm{alg}} = \SCL_\rho(R)$ (which we call the \emph{double semicircle law}), including efficient computation of a perfect solution for $R > 1/2$ in the balanced case.

\paragraph{Our results for OPI.}

Our first result for OPI improves the state-of-the-art for \cref{q:OPI}, giving an algorithmic result that outperforms even the best-known existential results.

\begin{restatable}[Quantum Algorithms for OPI]
{theorem}{mainalgo}
\label{thm:main-algo}
For any $R, \rho, \eps\in (0, 1)$, 
there exists a quantum algorithm with $\poly(n, \log p)$ running time that, given any OPI instance over $\F_p$ with rate $R$ and coherent membership oracle access to input lists $S_i$ where $|S_i|\ge \rho p$ for every $i$, has the following guarantees with high probability:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item Outputs a solution polynomial $Q$ with satisfaction ratio $s(Q)\ge \SCL_\rho(R)-\varepsilon$, 
    \item If $R+\rho>1$, outputs a solution polynomial $Q$ with $s(Q)=1$, i.e. $Q$ satisfies every constraint.
\end{enumerate}
\end{restatable}

\begin{remark}[Access model for the input lists]\label{rem:access}
    \cref{thm:main-algo} guarantees a quantum algorithm with membership oracle access to the lists $S_i$.  If the size of the field $\F_p$ is $p = \poly(n)$, then no membership oracles are needed, as the algorithm can simply take the lists $S_i$ as input and be its own oracle.  However, our results also work when $p$ is much larger than $\poly(n)$, in which case coherent membership oracle access is needed for an efficient algorithm. The precise computation model is similar to that of \cite{yz,hy} and is given in \cref{def:input-model}.
\end{remark}
We note that Item 2 of \cref{thm:main-algo} improves Item 1 in the case that $R + \rho > 1$: instead of a near-perfect solution (satisfying $1-\eps$ fraction of constraints for any $\eps>0$), we get a perfect solution that satisfies \emph{every} constraint. The result of \cref{thm:main-algo} is plotted in \cref{fig:opi-picture} for $\rho =1/2$ and $\rho = 1/4$.

