The algorithmic results in \cref{thm:main-algo} follow from improved existential results.  In more detail, the work \cite{sjo} showed how to make the existential results of \cite{us} algorithmic; we improve the existence results of \cite{us}, and use the techniques \cite{sjo} to arrive at \cref{thm:main-algo} above.  These improved existential results follow from improved results about optimal OPI discrepancy, which we state as our next main result below.

\begin{restatable}[Near-Optimal OPI Discrepancy for Reed--Solomon Codes]{theorem}{mainopi}
\label{thm:main-opi}
Fix any $\rho \in (0, 1)$. Every Reed--Solomon code of rate $R$ over a prime field has near-optimal OPI discrepancy for every instance with $|S_i|\ge \rho p$ for every $i$, provided that at least one of the following holds:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item $R>1/2$;
    \item  $R>1/2-\delta$ and $\rho\ge1/2-\delta$, for some absolute constant $\delta >0$;
    \item $R+\rho>1$.
\end{enumerate}
\end{restatable}
In particular, \cref{thm:main-opi} implies that, whenever $1,2$ or $3$ hold, there exists a perfect solution to OPI (provided that $R, \rho > 0$ are constants and that $n$ is sufficiently large).
This means that there are parameter regimes where \cref{thm:main-opi} guarantees the existence of a perfect solution, but where \cref{thm:main-algo} is only guaranteed to find one with partial satisfaction (see \cref{fig:phase}). 

Given the above, it is natural to ask if one can get better existential guarantees than the ones implied by the algorithmic double semicircle law of \cref{thm:main-algo}, even in parameter regimes when \cref{thm:main-opi} does not apply.  Indeed we can, and this is our next result.

\begin{restatable}[Existential improvement beyond the double semicircle law]
{theorem}{mainexistential}
\label{thm:main-existential}
Fix $\rho\in(0,1/2)$ and $\varepsilon>0$. For sufficiently large $n$, every Reed--Solomon code
$C\subset\F_p^n$ of fixed rate $R\in(\rho, 1/2]$ over a prime
field with $p\ge n$ admits, for every instance with $|S_i|\ge\rho p$
for all $i$, a solution $Q\in\F_p[X]$ with $ \deg Q < k$ with satisfaction ratio $s(Q)\ge V_\rho(R)-\varepsilon$, where
\begin{equation}
    V_\rho(R):=1-(1-2\rho)(1-2R).
\end{equation}
\end{restatable}

We remark that this bound improves over the double semicircle law guarantee throughout the region $R\in(\rho, 1/2]$, as
\[
    V_\rho(R)-\SCL_\rho(R)
    =
    \left(\sqrt{\rho(1-R)}
          -\sqrt{(1-\rho)R}\right)^2>0.
\]

We include a phase diagram in \cref{fig:phase} (over the phase space $(R, \rho)$) comparing \cref{thm:main-algo,thm:main-opi,thm:main-existential} over the whole parameter space.  The existential improvement of \cref{thm:main-existential} is not visible in \cref{fig:opi-picture}(a) because $V_\rho(R) - \SCL_\rho(R)\to 0$ as $\rho \to 1/2$; the improvement can be seen for example when $\rho=1/4$, shown in \cref{fig:opi-picture}(b).

