Finally, we turn our attention to the {classical} hardness of OPI.  It is known from~\cite{yz,folded-opi} that a similar problem to OPI---essentially the same problem, but defined for \emph{folded}\footnote{A folded RS code is a code $C \subset (\F_p^m)^N$, where the $i$'th symbol is $\vec{Q}(\alpha_i) := (Q(\alpha_i), Q(\gamma \alpha_i), \ldots, Q(\gamma^{m-1}\alpha_i)) \in \F_p^m$, where $\gamma \in \F_p^\times$ is a primitive element and where $\alpha_1, \ldots, \alpha_N \in \F_p^\times$ are distinct evaluation points so that $\gamma^a \alpha_i \neq \gamma^b \alpha_j$ for any $a,b \in \{0,\ldots, m-1\}$ and any $i \neq j \in [N]$.  Thus, the ``YZ problem'' studied by Yamakawa and Zhandry in \cite{yz} is, given oracle access to lists $S_1, \ldots, S_N \subset \F_p^m$, to find a polynomial $Q$ of degree less than $k$ so that $\vec{Q}(\alpha_i) \in S_i$ for all $i$.  The work \cite{folded-opi} proves a similar hardness result when a quantum algorithm may not find perfect solutions.  That is, they show that there is a quantum algorithm that can solve the folded OPI problem with some satisfaction ratio; but that any classical algorithm making only a polynomial number of queries to the membership oracles cannot exceed a smaller satisfaction ratio.} Reed--Solomon codes---is quantumly easy but classically hard (unconditionally) over very large folded alphabets, relative to a membership oracle for the lists $S_i$.\footnote{We note that a membership oracle is necessary, as in this parameter regime, the input lists $S_i$ are superpolynomial in $n$, and thus cannot be read as input efficiently.  A similar model is used in \cite{yz,hy,folded-opi}.}  Our next result adapts the hardness result of \cite{yz} to apply to OPI directly, rather than the folded version. 

\begin{restatable}[Quantum Advantage for OPI, Relative to a Membership Oracle---Informal, see \cref{thm:balanced-opi-advantage}]
{theorem}{mainadvantage}
\label{thm:main-opi-advantage}
There exists a random ensemble of balanced OPI instances of length $n$ with rate $1/\mathrm{polylog}(n)$ over prime fields $\F_p$ with quasipolynomial $p = \exp({\mathrm{polylog}(n)})$ 
such that the following hold, given access to a membership oracle for the input lists $S_i$:
\begin{itemize}
\item There is a polynomial-time quantum algorithm that finds a perfect solution with all but negligible probability.
\item Every randomized classical
algorithm making polynomially many queries to the membership oracle has negligible probability of outputting a solution with satisfaction ratio $\frac12+\Omega\left(\frac{1}{\log\log n}\right)$.
\end{itemize}
\end{restatable}
Here, the quantum algorithm has coherent access to the membership oracle, and the classical algorithm is allowed unrestricted computation
between queries. The probabilities are over the sampled instance from the ensemble and the algorithm, and the failure probabilities here are $e^{-\Omega((\log n)^2)}$. By tuning the parameters, one can obtain $e^{-\Omega(n^c)}$ probabilities for a constant $c>0$ at the cost of lower rate and classical satisfaction ratio control. The full theorem is stated in \cref{thm:balanced-opi-advantage} and this alternative parametrization is discussed in \cref{rem:alternative-param}.

\subsection{The relationship between LLR and OPI}

The relationship between LLR and OPI was first observed in \cite{us}.  At a high level, the connection is that similar \emph{Fourier proxies}---that is, quantities that bound the Fourier transform of the discrepancy $\left| \frac{|C \cap \mathbf{S}|}{|C|\prod_i \rho_i } - 1 \right|$ (for OPI) or the distance $\TV(L_{s\one},L_{s'\one})$ between two transcript distributions (for LLR)---can be used to solve both problems.  The work \cite{us} used this connection to import ideas from the LLR literature to make progress on OPI; but was not able to make progress on LLR itself.  In contrast, we make progress on both OPI and LLR, as outlined above.  In addition to this progress, we expand on the relationship between OPI and LLR.  

\paragraph{Our results on the relationship between LLR and OPI.}

We formalize the relationship observed by \cite{us}.  As in \cref{def:llr}, let $C \subset \F_p^n$ be the linear code $C = \{ (\alpha_i Q(\alpha_i))_{i=1}^n \,:\, \deg(Q) < k\}$, so that the LLR question concerns transcripts $(\ell_1(c_1), \ldots, \ell_n(c_n))$ when $c$ is chosen uniformly at random from a coset $s\one + C$.  It turns out that OPI is ``harder'' than LLR, in the sense that establishing near-optimal OPI discrepancy for the cosets $s\one + C$ will establish LLR for $C$.  We have the following theorem.

\begin{theorem}[OPI is at least as hard as LLR---Informal, see \cref{thm:opi-harder}]
\label{thm:informal-opi-harder}
Let $\ell_1, \ldots, \ell_n:\F_p \to \{0,1\}$ be one-bit leakage functions.  For $z \in \{0,1\}^n$, define $S_i(z_i) = \ell_i^{-1}(z_i)$.  Let 
$C = \{ \alpha_i \cdot Q(\alpha_i) \,:\, \deg(Q) < k \}$ be the generalized Reed--Solomon code in \cref{eq:secret-zero-code}.  Suppose that, for all $s \in \F_p$, and for all $z \in \{0,1\}^n$,  $s\one + C$ has near-optimal OPI discrepancy for input lists $S_1(z_1), \ldots, S_n(z_n)$.
Then $C$ is 1-bit local leakage resilient.
\end{theorem}
One might wonder if OPI is ``strictly'' harder than LLR; that is, does a converse to \cref{thm:informal-opi-harder} hold?  In fact, such a converse does not hold, and can fail to hold by up to an exponential factor, even for
fixed-rate GRS codes; see \cref{ex:opi-llr-strict}.

In the more formal version of the theorem (\cref{thm:opi-harder}), we show that more generally, if $v+C$ has near optimal OPI discrepancy for \emph{every} $v \in \F_p^n$, then $\TV(L_v, L_{v'})$ is small for \emph{every} $v,v' \in \F_p^n$.  Thus, LLR can be viewed as a restriction of near-optimal OPI discrepancy to just the cosets of $C$ given by constant shifts $s\one$.  In particular, any future improvements to near-optimal OPI discrepancy will imply improvements in LLR for Shamir sharing.\footnote{One subtlety is that in LLR, we typically assume that the $\rho_i$ can be arbitrary; while for OPI we assume that $\rho_i \geq \rho$ for some $\rho$, and the results are in terms of that $\rho$.  Thus, improvements of this sort for OPI will only lead to improvements of the same sort for LLR.  However, it is the case that, for example, improvements to the balanced case of OPI will imply improvements to the balanced case of LLR.}

\subsection{Technical Overview}
\label{subsec:overview}

In this section, we outline our approach.  At a high level, the outline is as follows:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Step 1: Define Fourier Proxies.}  As with most previous work on both LLR and OPI, we bound the quantity of interest---the discrepancy for OPI, and the TV distance between two transcript distributions for LLR---by taking a Fourier transform.  We then bound the Fourier transform by an easier-to-think-about expression that we call a \emph{Fourier proxy.}\footnote{The term ``Fourier proxy'' is borrowed from the literature on LLR.}  The observation of \cite{us} was that similar Fourier proxies can be used for both LLR and OPI.  For OPI, we use the same proxy that was used in \cite{us}.  For LLR, we use a similar Fourier proxy as in \cite{kk23}, except that we re-center and re-normalize to deal with arbitrary biases~$\rho_i$.
    After we define the Fourier proxies that we will use, we also prove (a generalization of) \cref{thm:informal-opi-harder}, which informally states that OPI is ``harder'' than LLR.
    \item \textbf{Step 2: Above rate 1/2, both LLR and OPI reduce to dual list-recovery; proving \cref{thm:main-llr}(1) and \cref{thm:main-opi}(1).} In coding theory, a code $C \subset \F_p^n$ is \emph{list-recoverable} if, given any input lists $S_1, \ldots, S_n \subset \F_p$ of size at most $\ell$, there are not too many elements $c \in C$ so that $c_i \in S_i$ for all but a $\delta$-fraction of $i$'s (see \cref{def:list-recovery}).  
    For LLR, the work \cite{mpsw21} implicitly showed that $b$-bit LLR for a code $C$ follows from list-recovery of $C^\perp$; combined with recent breakthroughs on the list-recovery of Reed--Solomon codes~\cite{bcpzz,gj26}, this immediately proves \cref{thm:main-llr}(1), the statement for LLR with rate approaching $1/2$.  Translating this result to OPI to prove \cref{thm:main-opi}(1) requires a more involved argument, discussed more below.
    \item \textbf{Step 3: Crossing the rate $1/2$ barrier; proving \cref{thm:main-llr}(2) and \cref{thm:main-opi}(2).}
    Our techniques from Step 2 cannot work below rate $1/2$, due to a result of~\cite{n24} about the limitations of the phase-forgotten Fourier proxy that we use.  To get past rate $1/2$---that is, to prove the statements \cref{thm:main-llr}(2) and \cref{thm:main-opi}(2)---we analyze the Fourier proxies (without forgetting the phases) and use Gowers norms to take advantage of phase cancellations.
    \item \textbf{Step 4: Non-perfect OPI solutions; proving \cref{thm:main-algo}(1) and \cref{thm:main-existential}.}  Step 3---in particular, \cref{thm:main-opi}(2)---establishes the existence of a \emph{perfect} solution for OPI  at rate slightly less than $1/2$.  However, \cref{thm:main-algo,thm:main-existential} give partial-satisfaction guarantees at lower rates. To show the existence of such solutions, we use the framework of \cite{us}, who propose a quantum state $|\varphi_\ell\rangle$ that, when measured, returns a good OPI solution with high probability.  We improve the analysis of this state, leveraging our earlier connection to list-recovery, as well as the results of \cite{bcpzz,gj26} on list-recovery of Reed--Solomon codes to capacity.  To make this result algorithmic and prove \cref{thm:main-algo}(1), we use a result of Jo in \cite{sjo}, which shows how to efficiently prepare the state $\ket{\varphi_\ell}$ provided that the dual code can be list-decoded efficiently up to a certain radius.  Recent algorithmic list-decoding results of \cite{bcpzz,dkt}, along with the existential results above, allow us to apply the algorithm of \cite{sjo} to prove \cref{thm:main-algo}(1). For \cref{thm:main-existential}, we leverage our framework to analyze a conditioned DQI distribution; our sampler does not efficiently prepare this conditioned distribution, but existentially it improves the double semicircle law to $V_\rho(R)$.
    \item \textbf{Step 5: OPI when $\rho + R  > 1$; proving \cref{thm:main-opi}(3) and \cref{thm:main-algo}(2).}  In this step we study the case when $R + \rho > 1$.  In this case, \cref{thm:main-algo}(1) already shows that an \emph{near}-perfect solution exists---that is, one satisfying a $1 - \eps$ fraction of the constraints, for any $\eps > 0$---but we need to show that a \emph{exactly} perfect solution exists (to prove \cref{thm:main-opi}(3)) and show how to find it efficiently (to prove \cref{thm:main-algo}(2)).  For this, we use a truncation argument, described more below, to be able to  apply our list-recovery-based framework.  Once we have the existential result, we can again use the ideas of \cite{sjo} to prove the algorithmic result \cref{thm:main-algo}(2).
    \item \textbf{Step 6: Classical hardness of OPI; proving \cref{thm:main-opi-advantage}.}  This step is technically separate from the rest of the paper, and can be read as a stand-alone section.  
    We give two scalar encodings of the
Yamakawa--Zhandry source problem~\cite{yz} to transform the folded Reed--Solomon hard instance it constructs into an (unfolded) Reed--Solomon instance. The first permits
arbitrary target rate but has sparse input lists. The second uses
balanced lists and a consistency argument, at the cost of a
vanishing target rate.
\end{itemize}

Before we expand on the technical details for the rest of this overview, we make a quick remark about the organization of the paper. 
\begin{remark}[Organization of the paper]\label{rem:org}
    We organize the technical overview into these six steps, as these are the six conceptual steps needed to prove our results.  However, we organize the paper itself slightly differently.  While Steps 2 and 3 are conceptually similar for LLR and OPI, the details differ, so we instead organize the paper to separate LLR and OPI, as follows. After some preliminaries in \cref{sec:prelim}, we complete Step 1 in \cref{sec:proxies}.  Then we complete Steps 2 and 3 for LLR in \cref{sec:llr}.  After that, we complete Steps 2 and 3 again for OPI, as well as the non-algorithmic part of Step 5, in \cref{sec:opi}.  We translate our combinatorial results into algorithmic results (Step 4 and the algorithmic part of Step 5) in \cref{sec:algo}.  Finally, we handle Step 6 in \cref{sec:advantage}.
\end{remark}

Now, we expand on the technical details of each of the six steps.  We begin by setting up some notation.  We use the same notation for both LLR and for OPI, by using $S_i$ for both the input lists in OPI and for the sets $S_i = \ell_i^{-1}(1)$ in LLR. 
In both cases, we have sets $S_i \subset \F_p$, and we define
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:def-gi}
g_i(x):=\frac{\one_{S_i}(x)-\rho_i}{\sqrt{\rho_i(1-\rho_i)}}.
\end{equation}

Throughout this discussion (and often in the paper going forward), we drop the polynomial notation $Q \in \F_p[X]$, referring instead to vectors $x \in C$, where $C$ is a Reed--Solomon code (possibly with no constant term, as in \cref{def:llr}).  Thus, for $x \in C$, $x_i$ represents the evaluation $Q(\alpha_i)$ of some appropriate polynomial $Q$.

\subsubsection*{Step 1: Define Fourier Proxies}
As mentioned above, we will bound the quantities we care about by \emph{Fourier proxies}, which we define here.  For OPI, fix $\mathbf{S} = S_1 \times \cdots \times S_n$, and let $C$ be a Reed--Solomon code.  Then the quantity that we care about (as in \cref{def:disc}) is
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:FO}
\FO(0)
:=\left|\frac{|C\cap \mbf{S}|}{|C|\prod_i\rho_i}-1\right|
=\left|\sum_{0\ne y\in C^\perp}
\prod_{i\in\supp(y)}\sqrt{\frac{1-\rho_i}{\rho_i}}\,\wh g_i(y_i)\right|,
\end{equation}
where the second equality follows from a Fourier transform over $\F_p$ (see \cref{subsec:fourier} for the Fourier conventions we use in this paper and \cref{lem:translated-opi-proxy} for a proof of \cref{eq:FO}).
Applying the triangle inequality to forget the phases gives the positive relaxation
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:FO+}
\FO^+
:=\sum_{0\ne y\in C^\perp}
\prod_{i\in\supp(y)}\sqrt{\frac{1-\rho_i}{\rho_i}}\,|\wh g_i(y_i)|
\ge\FO(0).
\end{equation}
Thus, to prove near-optimal OPI discrepancy for $C$ and $\mathbf{S}$, it suffices to show that either $\FO(0)$ or $\FO^+$ are exponentially small.  The Fourier proxy $\FO^+$ was used in \cite{us} to analyze OPI, and can be viewed a centered form of the Fourier proxy used in \cite{bdir}.

We contrast this with the following Fourier proxy for LLR. Recall that $L_{v}$ is the distribution on $\{0,1\}^n$ of the leakage transcript applied to uniform random vector in $v+C$. For $v=s\one$, this is the distribution of the leakage transcript  of the Shamir scheme, conditioned on secret $s$. 

For LLR, the quantity that we care about is $\TV(L_v, L_{v'})$.  We will bound this by the proxy
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:FL}
\FL(v,v')
:=
\frac14\sum_{\emptyset\ne A\subset[n]}
\left|
\sum_{\substack{y\in C^\perp\\\supp(y)=A}}
\left(e_p(-\langle v,y\rangle)-e_p(-\langle v',y\rangle)\right)
\prod_{i\in A}\wh g_i(y_i)
\right|^2.
\end{equation}

In \cref{lem:restated-llr-proxy}, we prove that
\begin{equation}\label{eq:llr-proxy}
\TV(L_v,L_{v'})^2\le\FL(v,v')\le\FL^+,
\end{equation} where $\FL^+$ is the positive relaxation 
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:FL+}
\FL^+
:=\sum_{\emptyset\ne A\subset[n]}
\left(\sum_{\substack{y\in C^\perp\\\on{supp} (y)=A}}\prod_{i\in A}|\wh g_i(y_i)|\right)^2.
\end{equation}
$\FL^+$ is similar to the proxy used by \cite{kk23}.  That argument obtained a rate threshold of $0.579$ for \emph{balanced} one-bit leakages.  For arbitrary biases, they obtain a threshold of $0.688$ via a reduction to the balanced case.  Our version can be viewed as a centered case for arbitrary biases, which lets us analyze the general case directly, which we do in \cref{subsec:llr-proxy}.  In fact, our results directly imply that the $0.579$ result of \cite{kk23} applies in the non-centered case too (see \cref{rem:bias-aware-kk}).

After we set up our Fourier proxies, we prove \cref{thm:informal-opi-harder}, which informally says that OPI is ``harder'' than LLR.  In addition to the statement in \cref{thm:informal-opi-harder} above, we also prove a statement at the level of Fourier proxies,  in the sense that the LLR proxies are bounded above by the OPI proxies.  

In more detail, write
$\mbf{S}_z:=\ell_1^{-1}(z_1)\times\cdots\times\ell_n^{-1}(z_n)$
and $\mbf{S}:=\mbf{S}_{\one}$. Then we show that
\[
\sqrt{\FL(s\one,s'\one;C,\mbf{S})}
\le
\max_{\substack{u\in\{s\one,s'\one\}\\z\in\{0,1\}^n}}
\FO(u;C,\mbf{S}_z),
\qquad
\sqrt{\FL^+(C;\mbf{S})}
\le
\max_{z\in\{0,1\}^n}\FO^+(C;\mbf{S}_z).
\]
The full statement---which includes the statement of \cref{thm:informal-opi-harder}, a generalization of it to arbitrary shifts, and the statement about about proxies---is given as \cref{thm:opi-harder}.

\subsubsection*{Step 2: Above rate 1/2, LLR and OPI reduce to dual list-recovery.}

In this step, we prove \cref{thm:main-llr}(1) and \cref{thm:main-opi}(1), which concern LLR and OPI discrepancy for any fixed rate $R > 1/2$.

We begin with LLR.  Our starting point is \cite{mpsw21}, which reduces $b$-bit LLR to conditions on the dual code.  They show that the \emph{Massey scheme} based on a \emph{random linear code}\footnote{This secret sharing scheme can be viewed as follows.  Choose a uniformly random matrix $G \in \F_p^{n \times k}$, and fix it; this defines the scheme.  Now, for a secret $s \in \F_p$, choose a random vector $r \in \F_p^k$ so taht $r_0 = s$.  Then the $n$ shares of $s$ are given by the $n$ elements of $G \cdot r$.} satisfies these conditions with high probability, provided the rate of the scheme is $R > 1/2$.  However, \cite{mpsw21} was not able to extend these results to Shamir sharing.

We observe in \cref{subsec:llr-above} that the condition identified in \cite{mpsw21} is implied by list-recovery of $C^\perp$.  For Shamir's scheme, $C^\perp$ is a \emph{generalized Reed--Solomon} (GRS) code.
Recent breakthroughs \cite{bcpzz,gj26} imply list-decoding and list-recovery up to capacity of GRS codes, which immediately proves \cref{thm:main-llr}(1). 

Thus, \cref{thm:main-llr}(1) follows essentially by assembling prior work as black boxes.  However, in order to apply these ideas to our other results, we need to un-black-box the role of list-recovery.  Our key observation is that the important consequence of list-recovery, which applies not just to LLR for $R > 1/2$ but also to our other results, is the following bound on weighted sums over the dual code.

\begin{restatable}[Weighted Fourier Thresholding]{lemma}{wftl}
\label{lem:wftl}
Fix constants $\gamma \in (0, 1)$ and $\Gamma>0$.  Let $p \geq n$ and let $C \subset \F_p^n$ be a generalized Reed--Solomon code of dimension $k$. Suppose that the weights $w_i:\F_p^\times \to \R_{\ge 0}$ for every $i\in [n]$ satisfies that
\[ \Vert w_i\Vert_\infty\le \gamma+o(1)\quad\text{and}\quad\Vert w_i\Vert_2^2=\sum_{\xi\ne 0}w_i(\xi)^2\le \Gamma+o(1)\] with both errors uniformly in $i$. Then, for every $t\in [n]$ with $2k-t=\Omega(n)$,
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:wftl}
\sum_{0\ne y\in C^\perp:|y|\le t} \prod_{i:y_i\ne 0} w_i(y_i)\le e^{o(n)} \gamma ^k=e^{-\Omega(n)}.
\end{equation}
\end{restatable}

We prove \cref{lem:wftl} in \cref{subsec:wftl}.
To see what this lemma has to do with LLR and OPI, observe that with $w_i =| \widehat{g_i}|$, the expression in \cref{lem:wftl} is similar to the expressions in the Fourier proxies $\FO^+$ in \cref{eq:FO+} and $\FL^+$ in \cref{eq:FL+}.  So the idea is to use it to bound those quantities, establishing LLR and near-optimal OPI discrepancy.  As a proof of concept, we show how to use \cref{lem:wftl} to prove a more quantitative version of \cref{thm:main-llr}(1) than follows as a black box from \cite{mpsw21,bcpzz,gj26}---see \cref{cor:one-bit-above}.  Next, we show how to use \cref{lem:wftl} to prove \cref{thm:main-opi}(1).  Going forward, we will also use \cref{lem:wftl} in Steps 3-5.

The proof idea for \cref{lem:wftl} is the following.
Fix a threshold $\theta > 0$.  Then, define $H_i=\{\xi\in\F_p^\times:w_i(\xi)\ge \theta\}$. List-recovery of $C^\perp$ (which for a GRS code follows from \cite{bcpzz,gj26}) implies that there are not too many codewords $y \in C^\perp$ with $y_i \in H_i$ for many $i$.  As there are not too many such $y$, they cannot contribute too much to the left-hand side of \cref{eq:wftl}.  On the other hand, the remaining codewords $y$ also have small contribution to the left-hand side of \cref{eq:wftl}, since for many coordinates $i$, we have $y_i \not\in H_i$, which means that $w_i(y_i) \leq \theta$.  Thus, the left-hand side of \cref{eq:wftl} cannot be too large.

Finally, we note an apparent barrier for this technique at $R=1/2$: for \cref{lem:wftl} to control every ``shell''  of $C^\perp$ (that is, the set $\{y \in C^\perp \,: \, |y| = t\}$ for each $t$), it needs to hold for weight $t=n$, which requires  $R>1/2$. This is further discussed in \cref{rem:wftl-param}.

\subsubsection*{Step 3: Crossing the rate-1/2 barrier.}

In this step, we prove \cref{thm:main-llr}(2) and \cref{thm:main-opi}(2), which concern LLR and OPI discrepancy for rates slightly smaller than $1/2$.

To understand our approach here, we explain a second apparent barrier at rate 1/2, which was identified by~\cite{n24} in the context of LLR.  That work constructs an example of leakage functions (or equivalently sets $S_i$) for which the phase-forgotten proxy $\FO^+$ is large for $R < 1/2$, but for which the value $\FO(0)$ that we actually care about is small.\footnote{We note that $\FO^+$ is our proxy for OPI, but \cite{n24} studies it in the context of LLR.  This is because this proxy is similar to one used by previous works in LLR~\cite{bdir,mpsw21,mnpw22}.  The leakage functions in \cite{n24} are based on quadratic residues.}
Thus a below-rate-1/2 result (for OPI at least) must retain the phases and take advantage of cancellation in the original expression $\FO(0)$ (and likely also in $\FL(v,v')$).  We do so with higher order Gowers norms---$U^3$ in particular---introduced in \cref{subsec:fourier}.

For the balanced case where $\rho\sim 1/2$, fix a sufficiently small $\delta>0$ and consider
$R\in[1/2-\delta,1/2]$. We split the terms in the sums over $y \in C^\perp$ in $\FO(0)$\footnote{Technically, we apply this to $\FO(v)$, which replaces $C$ with $C + v$ in $\FO(0)$.} and $\FL(v,v')$ by their weight, depending on whether $|y|$ is larger or smaller than 
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:puncture-size}
m:=\lfloor(2-\delta)k\rfloor
\end{equation}
For $y \in C^\perp$ of weight $|y| \leq m$,  we may apply \cref{lem:wftl} directly to $C^\perp$ (as $
    m\le 2k-\delta k$) to see their contributions to the relevant expressions are exponentially small. 

    On the other hand, suppose that $|y| > m$.  We focus on the LLR case for simplicity. Consider a support set $A$ for $y$ with $|A| > m$. Then the expression
    \begin{equation}\label{eq:wtb} \sum_{\substack{y \in C^\perp \\ \supp(y) = A}} e_p(-\langle v,y \rangle) \prod_{i \in A} \widehat{g_i}(y_j) \end{equation}
    that appears in $\FL(v,v')$ should be small; intuitively, this is because, if $A$ is large, then we should have lots of cancellations.  To formalize this, we use a version of the \emph{generalized von Neumann inequality} (\cref{lem:block-u3}), which bounds \cref{eq:wtb} in terms of the $U_3$ norms of the functions $g_i$.  Then we bound these norms $\|g_i\|_{U^3}$ in \cref{lem:u3-gap}, and this shows that \cref{eq:wtb} is small.  Plugging this into $\FL(v,v')$, we show that this too is small, as desired for our LLR result.  A similar argument works for $\FO$ in the case of OPI.

The approach above proves \cref{thm:main-llr}(2) and \cref{thm:main-opi}(2) in the balanced cases. We complete the proof to include arbitrary bias LLR and OPI with arbitrary density $\rho \geq 1/2 - \delta$ in \cref{sec:app-llr,sec:app-opi}.

The OPI extension uses subsampling from \cref{subsec:subsampling} The arbitrary-bias LLR extension instead uses the centered $L^1$ expansion and the Brascamp-Leib inequality to exploit strongly biased coordinates.

\subsubsection*{Step 4: Partial OPI satisfaction}
So far our results on OPI have concerned near-optimal OPI discrepancy, which can guarantee when a perfect solution exists, but doesn't say anything directly about imperfect solutions (that is, with satisfaction ratio less than $1$).
In this step, we prove \cref{thm:main-algo}(1) and \cref{thm:main-existential}.  The first says that  there is an efficient quantum algorithm that finds solution to OPI with satisfaction ratio about $\SCL_\rho(R)$ (the ``double semicircle law'').  The second says that there \emph{exist} solutions even beyond the double semicircle law when $\rho<R\le 1/2$. Both of these improved guarantees are less than $1$ for $R < 1- \rho$.

The idea is to consider the \emph{DQI state} $\ket{\varphi_\ell}$ proposed in \cite{dqi} to target a satisfaction ratio of $\SCL_\rho(\ell/n)$, where $\ell$ is a parameter we choose so that $\ell/n\le 1-\rho$. To define this state $\ket{\varphi_\ell}$, first define the \emph{$r$-wise discrepancy}\footnote{In contrast to \cite{us}, we define discrepancy in the ambient space of the code, $\F_p^n$, rather than the message space $\F_p^k$, to allow non-codewords.} of $x\in\F_p^n$ be
\begin{equation}
    q_r(x):= \sum_{A\in \binom{[n]}{r}} \prod_{i\in A}g_i(x_i) 
    \end{equation}
    where as before,
    $g_i(a) := \frac{\one _{S_i}(a)-\rho_i} {\sqrt{\rho_i(1-\rho_i)}}$.
    Fix some width $\sigma$ with $1\ll \sigma \ll \ell$, the DQI state is 
\begin{equation} 
\label{eq:dqi-u}
\ket{\varphi_\ell}\propto\sum_{x\in C}\sum_{r=0}^\ell u_rq_r(x) \ket{x}\quad\text{where}\quad u_r:=\binom{n}{r}^{-1/2}\one \{\ell - \sigma\le r\le \ell\}.
\end{equation}
    The algorithm is then to (1) prepare the state $\ket{\varphi_\ell}$, and (2) measure it to obtain codeword $x \in C$.  
With this approach, the analysis boils down to finding the largest $\ell$ such that:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item We can efficiently prepare the state $\ket{\varphi_\ell}$, and
    \item Measuring $\ket{\varphi_\ell}$ yields, on expectation, a good solution to the OPI instance.
\end{enumerate}

In \cite{dqi}, it was shown that for $\ell< \min\{k/2, (1-\rho)n\}$, Item (1) can be achieved by unique decoding of $C^\perp$, and that Item (2) holds using the fact that, with this choice of $\ell$, $2\ell +1\le k$ is below the distance of $C^\perp$ (which is $k+1$).  This choice of $\ell$ results in a quantum algorithm achieving a satisfaction ratio of at least $\SCL_\rho(\ell/n) = \SCL_\rho(R/2)$, which is the semicircle law.

In \cite{us}, it was shown that taking larger $\ell=k/2+\delta n$ is permissible for Item (2), provided one can bound an appropriate error term contributed from dual codewords with Hamming weight $t\in [k+1, \min\{n,2\ell+1\}]$. 
However, \cite{us} was not able to establish Item (1), and so their result was existential rather than algorithmic.  Follow-up work \cite{sjo} showed how to achieve Item (1) and efficiently prepare $\ket{\varphi_\ell}$, whenever $C^\perp$ can be list-decoded up to Hamming radius $\ell$. Using the Guruswami-Sudan algorithm, $\ket{\varphi_\ell}$ can be prepared up to any fixed slack below the \emph{Johnson bound} of $C^\perp$, which is $\ell = (1 - \sqrt{1-R})n$.
Thus, the work of \cite{sjo} established a quantum algorithm that recovers a state with expected satisfaction ratio $\SCL_\rho(\alpha)$ for any $\alpha < \min\{1-\sqrt{1-R}, R/2+\delta\}$ where the two cutoffs correspond to permissible $\ell$ for items (1) and (2) in \cite{sjo} and \cite{us}, respectively.\footnote{We note that the work \cite{hy} studied the same two items (1) and (2) for a related product state, but not for $\ket{\varphi_\ell}$ mentioned above, obtaining similar improvements (see \cref{sec:related_OPI}).  We follow the approach of \cite{us,sjo} in this work.}

To obtain our results in this step, we combine our list-recovery machinery developed above (in particular \cref{lem:wftl}) with the techniques from \cite{us,sjo}, and with the recent breakthroughs in algorithmic list-decoding and list-recovery of Reed--Solomon codes~\cite{bcpzz,gj26,dkt}.  For Item (1), preparing the state, we can use the result of \cite{sjo} as a black box, using the fact that \cite{bcpzz,dkt} implies that we may actually list-decode $C^\perp$ to radius approximately $R$ rather than $1 - \sqrt{1-R}$.  For Item (2), we use \cref{lem:wftl} (which itself uses the list-recovery result of \cite{gj26}) to control the error contributed by dual codewords with Hamming weight larger than $k$. Together, these ideas establish \cref{thm:main-algo}(1).

To prove \cref{thm:main-existential} and show the existence of solutions beyond the double semicircle law when $R \in (\rho, 1/2]$, we use an approach conceptually similar to Prange's algorithm.  In more detail, we choose a set $T \subset [n]$ of $t$ coordinates, and consider puncturing the relevant Reed--Solomon code to the coordinates indexed by $T$.  
We show that, whenever $t + 2\ell \leq 2k - \Omega(n)$, then conditioned on $Q(X)$ satisfying every remaining constraint indexed by $T$, its satisfaction ratio among the constraints in $[n] \setminus T$ is still given by the double semicircle law, with argument $\ell/(n-t)$.  

Together, the final satisfaction ratio is at least 
\[
\frac{t}{n}+\frac{n-t}{n} \on{SCL}_\rho\left(\frac{\ell}{n-t}\right).
\]
Then, optimizing $\ell, t$ as functions of $R$ gives $V_\rho(R)$, as claimed in \cref{thm:main-existential}. 

We emphasize this improvement is non-algorithmic, as discussed in \cref{rem:exist-no-algo}.

\subsubsection*{Step 5: OPI when $\rho+R>1$}
In this step, we prove \cref{thm:main-opi}(3) and \cref{thm:main-algo}(2), which both deal with the case when $\rho + R > 1$.  In this case, we establish near-optimal OPI discrepancy (which implies the \emph{existence} of a perfect solution), and show how to find that solution efficiently.

We begin with the existential result, \cref{thm:main-opi}(3).
As discussed in Step 3, the difficulty of OPI in the regime where $R<1/2$ is that \cref{lem:wftl} cannot apply to control Fourier errors from all shells of $C^\perp$ up to $t=n$.  However, when $R < 1/2$ but $\rho >1-R$, we can take advantage of the fact that the instance should intuitively be ``easy'' in that the $S_i$'s are very dense. Fix $\ell\sim \alpha n$ for $\alpha \in (1-\rho, R)$. For near-optimal OPI discrepancy, it suffices to show that for $\mathbf{S}:=S_1\times\dots \times S_n$
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:disc-check}
\mb{E}_{x\in C}[\rho^{-n}\one _{\mathbf{S}}(x)]=\mb{E}_{x\in C}[H(x)^2] = 1\pm e^{-\Omega(n)}\quad\text{where}\quad H(x):=\sum_{j=0}^n \rho^{(n-j)/2}(1-\rho)^{j/2}q_j(x).
\end{equation}
Indeed, the definition of near-optimal OPI discrepancy implies that we would like to show that $\E_{x \in C}[\rho^{-n} \mathbf{1}_{\mathbf{S}}(x)] = 1 \pm e^{-\Omega(n)}$; the expression in terms of $H$ follows by arithmetic manipulation (see \cref{eq:def-H}).
Inspired by \cite{hy}, we truncate the summands of $H(x)$ to those indexed by $j\le \ell$ to obtain $H_\ell$. 
We show that $H_\ell$ is a good proxy for $H$ on $\mbf{S}$: in particular, $H_\ell=(1-e^{-\Omega(n)})H$ pointwise on $\mbf{S}$, while its squared mass outside $\mbf{S}$ on $C$ is exponentially small (see  \cref{lem:key-truncation}).  Now, if we can show that
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:disc-proxy-check}
\mb{E}_{x\in C}[H_\ell^2]=1\pm e^{-\Omega(n)},
\end{equation}
this will imply \cref{eq:disc-check} and therefore near-optimal OPI discrepancy. 
To establish \cref{eq:disc-proxy-check}, we can again use our list-recovery-like lemma \cref{lem:wftl}. The key is that the truncation of $H_\ell$ means that we won't have to handle ``shells'' all the way up to $t = n$, which was the issue identified earlier; instead, it turns out that we only need to deal with shells $t \leq \min\{n, 2\ell + 1\} \leq 2k -\Omega(n)$ (see \cref{lem:key-truncation}).  This establishes \cref{eq:disc-proxy-check} and hence \cref{thm:main-opi}(3).

For a quantum algorithm to sample a perfect solution in this regime, we lower bound the probability that any particular perfect solution $\ket{x}$ is sampled when we measure $\ket{\varphi_\ell}$ for $\ell \sim (1-\rho)n$ by a direct computation.  Then we observe that these perfect states are abundant by the preceding paragraph.  Now, repeating the same algorithm as in Step 4 polynomially many times until we find a perfect solution will do the job, proving \cref{thm:main-algo}(2).

\subsubsection*{Step 6: Quantum Advantage of OPI}
To prove \cref{thm:main-opi-advantage}, we begin with the breakthrough result of
Yamakawa and Zhandry~\cite{yz}.  They  construct a high-rate folded Reed--Solomon code
$C_0\subset\Sigma^m$, where $\Sigma = \F_q^s$ for a prime power $q$ and some integer $s$, together with independent random oracles
$O_j:\Sigma\to\{0,1\}$, which determine the input lists $S_j$.  They show that there is a quantum algorithm that finds a perfect solution
$z\in C_0$ satisfying $O_j(z_j)=1$ for every $j$, whereas no
classical algorithm making polynomially many
queries can find a codeword with, say, $99\%$ satisfaction. See \cref{subsec:yz-recap} for more details.

We cast this construction as an OPI instance.  We are motivated by the fact that the recent list-decoding algorithms of \cite{bcpzz, dkt} can handle exponentially large fields (while still running in time polynomial in $n$, the length of the relevant Reed--Solomon code).  This means that a single scalar in a very large field can encode all $m$ coordinates of the Yamakawa-Zhandry instance. More specifically, we construct two types of input lists: $S_n$ gives an oracle that is equivalent to all $(O_1, \dots, O_m)$ simultaneously, so we can simulate the source oracles in our reductions.  Then we duplicate for $n-1$ times another oracle that encodes ``perfectly solving the Yamakawa-Zhandry instance''. This gives us polynomial time reductions so that quantumly solving the folded instance perfectly translates to solving the new unfolded instance perfectly; while classically it is hard to solve the folded instance and therefore hard to satisfy any of the $n-1$ constraints in the unfolded instance. This construction is spelled out in \cref{subsec:sparse-ensemble}.

This construction treats evaluations completely independently, as all the encoding happens at the scalar level. It therefore permits arbitrary target RS rate, but the key duplicated input list is
necessarily sparse: a random source message passes all $m$ independent
source constraints with probability $2^{-m}$.  In
\cref{subsec:balanced-ensemble}, we remove this sparsity by
distributing it into many small, exactly balanced parity tests.  The new
difficulty is ``consistency'', which relies on constraining the dimension of $C$ to require any polynomial $Q$ to present a consistent solution to each parity check. This gives the balanced input list but vanishing rate construction in \cref{subsec:balanced-ensemble} that proves \cref{thm:main-opi-advantage}.

\subsection{Related Work}
\label{subsec:literature}
Here, we further survey related work.  We begin with related work on local leakage resilience for Shamir's scheme.

\subsubsection{Local Leakage Resilience for Shamir's Scheme}\label{sec:related_LLR}
Local leakage resilience was introduced in \cite{bdir}, who were inspired by results in the regenerating codes literature~\cite{gw,tyb18} which implied that Shamir's scheme was \emph{not} LLR over extension fields.  The work \cite{bdir_conference} showed that Shamir's scheme (with arbitrary evaluation points) was $1$-bit LLR provided that $R = k/n$ satisfied $R > 0.907$.  Later, this was improved to $0.8675,0.85,0.78,0.69,0.668$ by \cite{mpsw21,bdir,mnpw22,kk23,k24}, respectively.  In the case of \emph{balanced} leakage functions, \cite{kk23} showed that $R > 0.58$ sufficed.  However, all of these works faced a barrier at $R = 1/2$.  This barrier was intrinsic in the technique used by \cite{bdir_conference,bdir,mpsw21,mnpw22}; in particular, the Fourier proxy that they bounded was not in fact small for $R < 1/2$ for certain one-bit leakage functions constructed in \cite{n24}, even though the term that this proxy bounds is indeed small.
Other work \cite{kk23} that used a different proxy did not face such a clear barrier at $R = 1/2$, but their techniques and proofs do seem to require $R > 1/2$.  Inspired by this, \cite{n24} asked if it was possible to beat the so-called ``$1/2$ barrier.'' That work showed that for certain leakage functions (in particular, \emph{quadratic residue} leakage), one could get LLR with arbitrarily small constant rate $R$.  However, the question of whether one could break or even approach the $R = 1/2$ barrier for \emph{worst-case} leakage functions remained open until our work.

We note that the work \cite{MNPSWYY22} did prove a result approaching the $1/2$ barrier, but this applied only to bounded classes of leakage functions and to Shamir schemes with {random} evaluation points.\footnote{In more detail, the result shows that, for any family of leakage functions $\mathcal{L}$ that is sufficiently small, then a Shamir scheme of rate $1/2 + \delta$ with random evaluation points, over a sufficiently large field, will be LLR to any leakage functions in $\mathcal{L}$ with high probability.}  Other works considered restricted classes of leakage functions, for example physical bits (rather than leaked bits that require non-trivial computation of the stored physical bits)~\cite{physical_leakage}.  In this work, we focus on the question of \emph{worst-case} families of leakage functions.

\subsubsection{Optimal Polynomial Intersection}\label{sec:related_OPI}
As mentioned earlier, questions related to OPI have been studied in other parameter regimes, most notably in the coding theory community as \emph{list-recovery} of Reed--Solomon codes~\cite{gs,GR06,LP20,lms25,LS25,bcdz,bcpzz,gj26}; similar problems have also been proposed (and refuted) as cryptographic hardness assumptions~\cite{np99,bn00,NP06}.  It is also related to the problem studied in the breakthrough work of Yamakawa and Zhandry~\cite{yz}, who show that a similar problem for \emph{folded} Reed--Solomon codes over exponentially large fields is classically hard but quantumly easy (relative to a membership oracle for the input lists).

In our main parameter regime (with polynomial field sizes), OPI was first studied by \cite{dqi}, who gave a quantum algorithm that finds a solution with satisfaction ratio up to $\SCL_\rho(R/2)$. It has since been the target of related quantum algorithms, including~\cite{chailloux1,chailloux2,khattar2025verifiable,rosmanis2026nearly,sjo,hy}, which are inspired by \emph{Regev's reduction}~\cite{regevReduction}.

The works \cite{chailloux1,chailloux2} gave algorithms to find solutions in a not-quite-worst-case model up to $\SCL_\rho(\alpha_J(R))$, where $\alpha_J(R) = 1 - \sqrt{1 -R}$ is the Johnson radius.  More precisely, in this model the input list begin as worst-case $\tilde{S}_i \subset \F_p$, except that they are then translated by a random field element $v_i \in \F_p$ to obtain $S_i = \tilde{S}_i + v_i$.  This result guarantees an algorithmic solution (with high probability over the input lists) provided that $R > 0.75$.

The work \cite{us} improved the existential bound in the worst-case setting, guaranteeing the existence of solutions with satisfaction ratio $\SCL_\rho(R/2 + \delta)$ for some $\delta > 0$; in particular, this implied the existence of an asymptotically perfect solution whenever $R \geq 0.749$, slightly smaller than the $0.75$ guaranteed by \cite{chailloux2} (although not algorithmic).  We note that \cite{us} also observed the key connection between OPI and LLR that we exploit, namely that similar Fourier proxies can be used to get a handle on both problems (although their improvements were not enough to improve on the state-of-the-art for LLR, only for OPI).  We make their observations more precise in \cref{thm:informal-opi-harder}.  

