\begin{abstract}
The three gauge couplings of the Standard Model (SM)  can be parameterized as the fine structure constant $\alpha$, the weak mixing angle $\sin^2\theta_w$, and the strong coupling $\alpha_s$. They are fundamental constants whose values remain unexplained. Previously we showed that minimizing  magic production in charged-lepton scattering reproduces $\sin^2\theta_w$ with great precision. Here we study magic production in the six flavor-diagonal color-singlet channels $q\bar q\to q\bar q$ at tree level, including photon, gluon, $W/Z$, and Higgs exchange. Varying only $\alpha_s$, with all other inputs fixed at each center-of-mass energy, we find that the finite-$\alpha_s$ magic minimum in the top channel reproduces the $\overline{\mathrm{MS}}$ value of $\alpha_s$ to within $5\%$ from  $1$~TeV to $10^9$~GeV. Higgs exchange involving the large top Yukawa coupling is essential for this agreement. All six channels produce near-minimal magic at the SM couplings, and reducing $\alpha_s$ toward $\alpha$ increases their magic production, potentially explaining why the strong interaction is ``strong.''  To understand why minimizing magic reproduces both $\sin^2\theta_w$ and $\alpha_s$, we conjecture that the parameters of fundamental interactions among color singlets reflect a principle of quantum computational efficiency, with the physical universe emerging from a quantum simulation subject to constrained resources.
\end{abstract}
\maketitle

\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:intro}
The gauge interactions of the Standard Model (SM) are determined by the symmetry group $SU(3)_c\times SU(2)_L\times U(1)_Y$ with coupling constants $g_3$, $g_2$ and $g_1$, respectively. The coupling strengths define three of the most important fundamental constants in nature: the electric charge $e\equiv g_1g_2/\sqrt{g_1^2+g_2^2}$, the weak mixing angle $\sin^2\theta_w \equiv g_1^2/(g_1^2+g_2^2)$ and the strong coupling $g_s\equiv g_3$.  Their values are unexplained and precisely measured experimentally at several energy scales~\cite{ParticleDataGroup:2024cfk}. 

In Ref.~\cite{Liu:2025bgw} we initiated a study of whether the observed couplings are special from the perspective of quantum computation. The relevant resource is non-stabilizerness, or magic, introduced by Bravyi and Kitaev~\cite{Bravyi:2004isx}. By the Gottesman--Knill theorem~\cite{Gottesman:1998hu,Gottesman:1999tea,Aaronson:2004xuh}, circuits built from Clifford gates acting on stabilizer states can be simulated efficiently on a classical computer even when they generate maximal entanglement, so entanglement alone is insufficient for a quantum speedup, motivating magic as an additional computational resource. In Ref.~\cite{Liu:2025bgw}, minimizing the production of magic in charged-lepton scattering gave a weak mixing angle within a percent of its measured $\overline{\rm MS}$ value at around the TeV scale. Because an overall factor of the scattering amplitude drops out of the normalized outgoing spin state at tree level and at fixed center-of-mass (CM) energy,  the magic production in this case  depends on the gauge couplings only through $\sin^2\theta_w$.

The quantum-information perspective on fundamental interactions has by now a substantial literature. The emergence of symmetries from the suppression or maximization of entanglement or magic in scattering has been studied in a variety of settings~\cite{Beane:2018oxh,Low:2021ufv,Liu:2022grf,Carena:2023vjc,Liu:2023bnr,Hu:2024hex,Chang:2024wrx,Kowalska:2024kbs,McGinnis:2025brt,Busoni:2025dns,Carena:2025wyh,Hu:2025lua,McGinnis:2025iab,McGinnis:2025xgt,Li:2026kha,Low:2026evp,Li:2026udy,Kanemura:2026won,Low:2026oyf, Low:2026kxb}. An area law relates the entanglement entropy to the elastic cross section~\cite{Aoude:2024xpx,Low:2024mrk,Low:2024hvn,Liu:2025pny}. Entanglement has also been related to SM parameters, as in $\sin^2\theta_w=1/4$ from maximal entanglement~\cite{Cervera-Lierta:2017tdt}, flavor mixing from entanglement minimization~\cite{Thaler:2024anb}, and parameter inference in Higgs decays~\cite{Liu:2025iwh}. Magic production has been computed for processes in nuclear and particle physics~\cite{Robin:2024oqc,Li:2026udy,Chernyshev:2024pqy,Liu:2025qfl,White:2024nuc,Aoude:2025jzc,Gargalionis:2025iqs,Gargalionis:2026onv,Chu:2026yxm,Alvarado:2026owd,Viaux:2026skf,Cao:2026aye}, alongside studies of maximal and nonlocal magic~\cite{Liu:2025frx,Roman:2026mcy,Knipfer:2026ixm,Robin:2025dgw,Busoni:2026lvp}. Notably, the magic produced depends on the spin basis, since the rotation from laboratory to helicity bases is generally not a Clifford operation, but the physical weak mixing angle lies close to the magic-minimizing value in both bases~\cite{Li:2026zum}. These results motivate computing the magic production in quark scatterings,  where the strong, electroweak, and Yukawa interactions all contribute.

\begin{figure*}[t]
 \centering

 \caption{Tree diagrams for flavor-diagonal color-singlet $q\bar q\to q\bar q$ scattering. Notice that the $s$-channel gluon exchange vanishes in the color singlet channel.}
 \label{fig:diagrams}
\end{figure*}

In this work we study elastic quark scattering at tree level, focusing on the six flavor-diagonal color singlets $q\bar q\to q\bar q$ (Fig.~\ref{fig:diagrams}). Gluon exchange introduces an additional gauge-coupling ratio, $\alpha_s/\alpha$, in the normalized spin state, beyond the weak mixing angle probed in charged-lepton scattering. Following Ref.~\cite{Liu:2025bgw}, we use the laboratory (Lab) basis and vary only $\alpha_s$, holding all other inputs fixed. Near the TeV scale $\alpha_s/\alpha\simeq11$, and the normalized spin states of the light-quark channels are close to those produced by gluon exchange alone, so that their magic depends only weakly on $\alpha_s$ near the SM value. Near-minimal magic in these channels therefore does not by itself determine $\alpha_s$ precisely. In the top channel, however, the large top Yukawa coupling allows Higgs exchange to compete with gluon exchange. Physical Higgs exchange is essential for a deep, finite-$\alpha_s$ magic minimum whose location reproduces the $\overline{\rm MS}$ running $\alpha_s$ to within a few percent from about $1\TeV$ to $10^8\GeV$. At higher CM energies gluon exchange becomes less dominant, with $\alpha_s/\alpha\simeq2.5$ at $10^{16}\GeV$, yet all six diagonal channels remain close to their magic minima. Moreover, reducing $\alpha_s$ to $\alpha$ increases their magic production, offering a potential explanation for why the strong interaction is stronger than electromagnetism. Flavor-nondiagonal and nonsinglet channels show a less uniform pattern.

\section{Stabilizer R\'enyi entropy}
\label{sec:magic}
Characterizing and quantifying magic is an active subject in quantum computation~\cite{Veitch:2012ttw,Mari:2012ypq,Veitch:2013bll,Bravyi:2018ugg,Liu:2019qfi,Liu:2020yso,Warmuz:2024cft}. Here we briefly review the measure used below, the stabilizer R\'enyi entropy (SRE)~\cite{Leone:2021rzd}, which is convenient to compute, can be measured experimentally~\cite{Oliviero:2022bqm,Haug:2023ffp}, and, for integer order $\alpha\ge2$, is a monotone under deterministic pure-state stabilizer protocols~\cite{Veitch:2013bll,Chitambar:2018rnj,Leone:2024lfr,Bittel:2025yhq}. For a normalized pure state of two qubits, the second SRE is
\begin{equation}
 M_2(\ket{\psi})=-\ln\!\left[\frac14\sum_{P\in\{I,X,Y,Z\}^{\otimes2}}
 \bigl(\bra{\psi}P\ket{\psi}\bigr)^4\right].
 \label{eq:magic}
\end{equation}
Here $P=\sigma_i\otimes\sigma_j$ runs over the sixteen two-qubit Pauli strings, where $\sigma_i\in\{I,X,Y,Z\}$ are the identity and the three Pauli matrices; see Refs.~\cite{Liu:2025qfl,Liu:2025frx} for brief reviews. Equation~\eqref{eq:magic} vanishes on stabilizer states and is invariant under Clifford transformations. We use the natural logarithm; the maximum of $M_2$ over two-qubit states is $\ln(16/7)\simeq0.827$~\cite{Liu:2025frx}. There are sixty pure two-qubit stabilizer states: thirty-six product states and twenty-four maximally entangled states. Their explicit construction is given in Appendix~A of Ref.~\cite{Li:2026zum}. Averaging the outgoing magic over this set provides a common comparison of scattering amplitudes whose inputs have no magic.

\section{Gauge couplings and running}
\label{sec:running}
With $e$, $\sin^2\theta_w\equiv s_w^2$, and $g_s$ defined in the beginning of Sec.~\ref{sec:intro}, the variable we scan is
\begin{equation}
 \rs\equiv\frac{g_s^2}{e^2}=\frac{\alpha_s}{\alpha}.
 \label{eq:couplings}
\end{equation}
We use the full-SM gauge and Yukawa couplings in the $\overline{\mathrm{MS}}$ scheme at the renormalization scale $\mu=\sqrt{s}$, the CM energy. These running couplings, together with the mass prescription specified below, define our renormalization-group-improved tree-level calculation. The one-loop evolution of the gauge couplings is
\begin{equation}
 \mu \frac{dg_i}{d\mu}=b_i \frac{g_i^3}{16\pi^2},\quad
 (b_1,b_2,b_3)=\left(\frac{41}{6},-\frac{19}{6},-7\right),
 \label{eq:running}
\end{equation}
where $g_1$ is the hypercharge coupling in its conventional normalization, without assuming grand unification~\cite{ParticleDataGroup:2026mpi}. We start the running at $\mu_0=91.188\GeV$, chosen equal to the input $Z$-boson mass. Equation~\eqref{eq:running} treats the full SM, including the top quark, as active from $\mu_0$, so its initial conditions are the six-flavor $\overline{\rm MS}$ couplings. We take $g_1$, $g_2$, and $g_3$ at $\mu_0$ from Ref.~\cite{Antusch:2025fpm}, where they are obtained with SMDR~\cite{Martin:2019lqd} from PDG~2024 inputs~\cite{ParticleDataGroup:2024cfk}; Table~\ref{tab:inputs} lists these and all other inputs. They correspond to $\alpha^{-1}(\mu_0)=128.11$, $s_w^2(\mu_0)=0.2315$, and $\alpha_s(\mu_0)=0.1170$, the last being the six-flavor counterpart of the top-decoupled value $\alpha_s^{(5)}(\mu_0)=0.1180$ used by the Particle Data Group (PDG).

