The three-generation Yukawa matrices are evolved with the full one-loop SM running in the $\overline{\mathrm{MS}}$ scheme~\cite{Antusch:2005gp,Kielanowski:2008wm}. We use the convention $Y_f=U_{Lf}\,\mathrm{diag}(y_f)\,U_{Rf}^{\dagger}$, so that the CKM matrix is $V=U_{Lu}^{\dagger}U_{Ld}$. The Yukawa couplings at $\mu_0$ are also taken from Ref.~\cite{Antusch:2025fpm}. The CKM matrix at $\mu_0$ is written in the standard unitary parametrization~\cite{Chau:1984fp}, with $s_{13}$, $s_{23}$, and $\delta$ from Ref.~\cite{Antusch:2025fpm} and $c_{12}=|V_{ud}|/c_{13}$ fixed by the measured $|V_{ud}|$ (Table~\ref{tab:inputs}). This choice defines a unitary benchmark combining the two input sources and gives $|V_{us}|=0.22793$. We define the running fermion and vector masses by
\begin{equation}
 \begin{gathered}
 m_f(\mu)=\frac{y_f(\mu)v}{\sqrt2},\qquad M_W(\mu)=\frac{g_2(\mu)v}{2},\\
 M_Z(\mu)=\frac{v}{2}\sqrt{g_2^2(\mu)+g_1^2(\mu)},
 \end{gathered}
 \label{eq:masses}
\end{equation}
with a fixed electroweak normalization $v=246.925\GeV$. We choose $v=2M_W^{\mathrm{input}}/g_2(\mu_0)$ so that the running $W$ mass reproduces the measured input at $\mu=\mu_0$. The $Z$-boson mass entering the amplitudes follows Eq.~\eqref{eq:masses}, giving $M_Z(\mu_0)=91.678\GeV$. The common $v$ in Eq.~\eqref{eq:masses} preserves the tree-level mass--coupling relations required for a consistent treatment of longitudinal $W/Z$ exchange and Yukawa interactions. Defining running fermion masses through running Yukawa couplings and a fixed electroweak normalization is an established convention~\cite{Fusaoka:1998vc,Xing:2007fb,Antusch:2013jca}, where the scale dependence of the masses in Eq.~\eqref{eq:masses}  comes entirely from the Yukawa and gauge couplings. These running masses are used consistently in external spinors, kinematics, and propagators.


Unless stated otherwise, we use the physical Higgs mass, $m_h=125.20\GeV$, as a fixed propagator input. The Higgs quartic coupling does not enter these tree amplitudes independently and does not appear in the one-loop evolution of the gauge and Yukawa couplings. Thus the running scalar potential is not needed for the prescription adopted here. The Higgs mass enters only through the propagator denominators.  All scattering amplitudes are evaluated at tree level with zero widths.

At each $\sqrt{s}$ we first evaluate the SM reference values of the running couplings, the CKM matrix, and the running masses of Eq.~\eqref{eq:masses} at $\mu=\sqrt{s}$. We then vary only $\alpha_s$, or equivalently $\rs$ since $\alpha$ is fixed. All other inputs, including $y_t$, the CKM matrix, and the masses, remain fixed throughout this scan. The SM point is $\rs^{\SM}=\alpha_s(\mu)/\alpha(\mu)$. 

We display the coupling curves through $\rs=100$ only to show their approach to the pure-QCD limit, $\rs\to\infty$, which is equivalent to turning off all other couplings. We shade the region $\alpha_s>0.25$ as an illustrative benchmark for larger couplings, where higher-order corrections may become sizable. The shaded region begins at
\begin{equation}
 \rs^{(0.25)}=\frac{0.25}{\alpha(\mu)}.
 \label{eq:shade}
\end{equation}
The size of loop corrections  depends on the observable, kinematics, and renormalization scheme; this benchmark only serves as a visual reminder in the figures. The minimum of the averaged magic, $\Mtwo_{\min}(\sqrt{s})$ defined in Eq.~\eqref{eq:comparison}, is taken over the entire range $\rs\ge0$, including the asymptotic limit $\rs\to\infty$; neither the shading nor the displayed range truncates this minimization. In the asymptotic limit, the overall strong-coupling factor cancels from the normalized spin state. The resulting magic then arises purely from QCD, without the electroweak and Yukawa interactions.

\section{Magic in quark scattering}
\label{sec:scattering}

\begin{figure}[t]
 \centering

 \caption{Magic production  in the $t\bar t$ color singlet as a function of $\alpha_s$ at $\sqrt{s}=1\TeV$, $10\TeV$, and $10^8\GeV$, with all other inputs fixed according to Sec.~\ref{sec:running}. Circles and thin vertical lines mark the SM value $\alpha_s(\sqrt{s})$. Arrows at the right edge mark the pure-QCD limit $\alpha_s\to\infty$.}
 \label{fig:ttcurves}
\end{figure}

Our choice of reference frame for spin projection is the Lab basis defined in Ref.~\cite{Liu:2025qfl}, where $\hat z$ is along the incoming quark, $\hat x$ lies in the scattering plane, and $\hat y$ is normal to it, with the axes oriented so that the outgoing quark has azimuthal angle $\phi=0$. In the Lab basis amplitudes and magic then depend on $\theta$ only. For both incoming and outgoing particles, the computational basis of qubits is defined by the spin projection onto the common $\hat z$ axis.

For each diagonal flavor we prepare and project the coherent color singlet $\ket{1}=\sum_{i=1}^3\ket{i\bar i}/\sqrt3$. The resulting spin amplitude $A(c_\theta;\rs,\sqrt{s})$, with $c_\theta\equiv\cos\theta$, includes all interfering diagrams in Fig.~\ref{fig:diagrams}. In the  singlet channel the color factors are $C_F=4/3$ for $t$-channel gluon exchange, and $1$ and $3$ for the exchange of a colorless boson in the  $t$-channel and  $s$-channel, respectively. The $s$-channel gluon exchange vanishes in the  singlet channel,  since a color-singlet $q\bar q$ pair cannot annihilate into a color-octet gluon. The dependence on $\alpha_s/\alpha$ thus comes entirely from the $t$-channel gluon term. The massive $W/Z$ contributions are evaluated in the unitary gauge.

For an initial stabilizer state $\ket{\psi_s}_i$, $i=1,\dots,60$, the spin configuration of the outgoing quarks at the scattering angle $\theta$ is given by the color-singlet spin amplitude and represented by the state vector $\ket{\tilde\psi}=A\ket{\psi_s}_i$. Since fixing the angle $\theta$ amounts to a projective measurement of the outgoing momentum direction, $\ket{\tilde\psi}$ is not normalized, and we compute the magic from $\ket{\psi}=\ket{\tilde\psi}/\langle\tilde\psi|\tilde\psi\rangle^{1/2}$, denoted $M_2(\ket{\psi_s}_i,\theta)$.
 Following Ref.~\cite{Liu:2025bgw}, we average over the sixty stabilizer states and the solid angle,
\begin{align}
 M_2(\theta)&\equiv\frac1{60}\sum_{i=1}^{60}M_2(\ket{\psi_s}_i,\theta),\notag\\
 \Mtwo(\rs;\sqrt{s})&\equiv\frac1{4\pi}\int M_2(\theta)\,\sin\theta\,d\theta\,d\phi,
 \label{eq:average}
\end{align}
where the dependence of $M_2(\theta)$ on $\rs$ and $\sqrt{s}$ is left implicit. The angular average is uniform in solid angle and is not weighted by the differential cross section. 

We proceed to minimize Eq.~\eqref{eq:average} with respect to $r_s$ over the full coupling range, including its asymptotic limit. At each CM energy the minimum and percentage excess at the SM point are
\begin{align}
 \Mtwo_{\min}(\sqrt{s})&\equiv\inf_{\rs\ge0}\Mtwo(\rs;\sqrt{s}),\notag\\
 \delta_{\Mtwo}(\sqrt{s})&\equiv100\,\frac{\Mtwo(\rs^{\SM};\sqrt{s})-\Mtwo_{\min}(\sqrt{s})}{\Mtwo_{\min}(\sqrt{s})}.
 \label{eq:comparison}
\end{align}
In the following we begin with the top channel, where the Higgs exchange contribution is comparable to that from the gluon exchange, and turn to the survey of all six diagonal channels in Sec.~\ref{sec:diagonal}.

\subsection{The top channel and the strong coupling}
\label{sec:top}

\begin{figure}[t]
 \centering

 \caption{Magic-minimizing $\alpha_s$ in the $t\bar t$ color singlet as a function of $\sqrt{s}$, compared with the SM running coupling (dash-dotted). Filled circles: global minimum. Open circles:  finite $\alpha_s$ minimum, as the pure-QCD limit is lower. The lower panel shows the ratio to the SM value; the dashed lines mark $\pm5\%$.}
 \label{fig:alphas}
\end{figure}

Figure~\ref{fig:ttcurves} shows the magic in the $t\bar t$ singlet as a function of $\alpha_s=\alpha\,\rs$ at $\sqrt{s}=1\TeV$, $10\TeV$, and $10^8\GeV$. At each CM energy the SM  $\alpha_s(\sqrt{s})$ sits very close to the finite $\alpha_s$ minimum. At $10\TeV$ and $10^8\GeV$ the finite $\alpha_s$ minimum lies below the pure-QCD limit and is therefore the global minimum of Eq.~\eqref{eq:average}. At $1\TeV$ the finite $\alpha_s$ minimum is slightly above the pure-QCD limit, and the magic falls below it only for $\alpha_s>0.36$, a somewhat large coupling. The additional  structures at smaller $\alpha_s$ arise from interference among different Feynman diagrams. 

In Fig.~\ref{fig:alphas} we show the location of the finite $\alpha_s$ minimum from $1\TeV$ to $10^{16}\GeV$, together with the SM running coupling.  From $1.3\TeV$ it is the global minimum on the whole range $\rs\ge0$ (filled circles); between $1$ and $1.2\TeV$ the pure-QCD limit is lower (open circles), but the magic falls below the basin only for $\alpha_s$ above $0.36$ to $0.67$. Below $1\TeV$, where $2m_t/\sqrt{s}$ exceeds $0.3$, the structure changes: below $0.8\TeV$ a dip at $\alpha_s\approx0.03$ is deeper within $\alpha_s\le0.25$, and below $0.55\TeV$ there is no minimum near the SM point. Interestingly, the magic-minimizing $\alpha_s$ lies below the SM value at every CM energy above 1 TeV, as can be seen from Fig.~\ref{fig:alphas}. The relative deviation is $100(\alpha_s^{\min}/\alpha_s^{\SM}-1)$, which is only a few percent between  $\sqrt{s}=1$ TeV and $10^8$ GeV.

