Higgs exchange is essential for this result. At $1\TeV$ and $m_h=125.20\GeV$, the magic at the SM value of $\alpha_s$ is $0.298384$, close to the global minimum $0.290433$ at the pure-QCD limit. In the limit $m_h\to\infty$, the magic at the same value of $\alpha_s$ rises to $0.335687$, while the minimum is $0.227095$ at $\alpha_s=0$. The corresponding excesses are
\begin{equation}
 \begin{aligned}
 m_h=125.20\GeV:&\quad \delta_{\Mtwo}=2.7\%,\\
 m_h\to\infty:&\quad \delta_{\Mtwo}=47.8\%.
 \end{aligned}
 \label{eq:top}
\end{equation}
Removing the Higgs here means taking the $m_h\to\infty$ limit at each fixed finite CM energy, with all other parameters held fixed. This limit removes both the $t$- and $s$-channel physical-Higgs exchange diagrams, while leaving all other contributions unchanged. The $t$- and $s$-channel diagrams contribute with opposite sign: removing only the $s$-channel Higgs exchange raises the SM-point magic to $0.348331$, whereas removing only the $t$-channel lowers it to $0.288710$. The SM result reflects both contributions together.

Figure~\ref{fig:higgs} extends the comparison to higher CM energies. At the physical Higgs mass, $\delta_{\Mtwo}$ stays below $3\%$ at all sampled CM energies from $1\TeV$ to $10^{12}\GeV$. Taking the $m_h\to\infty$ limit, it lies between $26\%$ and $48\%$ up to $10^{12}\GeV$,  with the magic minimum always sitting at $\alpha_s=0$; at $10^{16}\GeV$ it is $11.5\%$, measured from the pure-QCD limit.

\subsection{Light flavor-diagonal singlets}
\label{sec:diagonal}

In this subsection we consider the remaining five flavor-diagonal color singlets.  Fig.~\ref{fig:lightcurves}  shows the magic production as a function of $\rs=\alpha_s/\alpha$  for the $u\bar u$ and $b\bar b$ singlets, representing the up-type and down-type quarks.  The $c\bar c$ curves are almost identical to $u\bar u$, differing  by at most $5\times10^{-5}$ in $\Mtwo$,  while the $d\bar d$ and $s\bar s$ curves differ from $b\bar b$  by at most $2\times10^{-4}$ for $\rs>1$ and $2.4\times10^{-3}$ at smaller $\rs$, except near the sharp feature at $\rs\approx0.05$ to $0.09$, where the difference is approximately $1.2\times10^{-2}$. 

The two panels show two qualitatively different behaviors. The $b\bar b$ singlet, the weak-isospin partner of the top, descends from a sharp feature at small $\rs$ to a shallow finite minimum slightly below the pure-QCD value at all three CM energies; the SM magic exceeds these minima by at most $0.18\%$. The up-type channels approach the pure-QCD value from above, and their shallow local dips lie above it, so that their minimum is the pure-QCD limit. 

At CM energies from $1\TeV$ to $10^{12}\GeV$, the five lighter flavors lie within $1\%$ of their minima. At $10^{16}\GeV$ the $u\bar u$ and $c\bar c$ excesses reach $1.38\%$, while the three down-type channels remain below $0.18\%$. For these five flavors, physical Higgs exchange changes the excess by at most $0.0012$ percentage points, in contrast to the top channel of Sec.~\ref{sec:mechanism}. 

